it provides a good summary of the different similarity measures  their respective formulas  and applications  in contrast to the cosine  the dot product is proportional to the vector length  this is important because examples that appear very frequently in the training set  for example  popular youtube videos  tend to have embedding vectors with large lengths  if you want to capture popularity  then choose dot product  however  the risk is that popular examples may skew the similarity metric  to balance this skew  you can raise the length to an exponent    1 to calculate the dot product as  a   b  cos         to better understand how vector length changes the similarity measure  normalize the vector lengths to 1 and notice that the three measures become proportional to each other 
