this is a brief introduction to what is vector space model   sound  this lecture is about the vector space retrieval model  we re going to give an introduction to its basic idea   play video starting at   18 and follow transcript0 18  in the last lecture  we talked about the different ways of designing a retrieval model  which would give us a different arranging function   play video starting at   30 and follow transcript0 30  in this lecture  we re going to talk about a specific way of designing a ramping function called a vector space retrieval model   play video starting at   37 and follow transcript0 37  and we re going to give a brief introduction to the basic idea   play video starting at   44 and follow transcript0 44  vector space model is a special case of similarity based models as we discussed before  which means we assume relevance is roughly similarity  between the document and the query   play video starting at  1 2 and follow transcript1 02  now whether is this assumption is true is actually a question  but in order to solve the search problem  we have to convert the vague notion of relevance into a more precise definition that can be implemented with the program analogy  so in this process  we have to make a number of assumptions  this is the first assumption that we make here  basically  we assume that if a document is more similar to a query than another document  then the first document will be assumed it will be more relevant than the second one  and this is the basis for ranking documents in this approach   play video starting at  1 46 and follow transcript1 46  again  it s questionable whether this is really the best definition for randoms  as we will see later there are other ways to model randoms   play video starting at  1 58 and follow transcript1 58  the basic idea of vectors for base retrieval model is actually very easy to understand  imagine a high dimensional space where each dimension corresponds to a term   play video starting at  2 11 and follow transcript2 11  so here i issue a three dimensional space with three words  programming  library and presidential  so each term here defines one dimension   play video starting at  2 24 and follow transcript2 24  now we can consider vectors in this  three dimensional space  and we re going to assume that all our documents and the query will be placed in this vector space  so for example  on document might be represented by this vector  d1  now this means this document probably covers library and presidential  but it doesn t really talk about programming  what does this mean in terms of representation of document  that just means we re going to look at our document from the perspective of this vector  we re going to ignore everything else  basically  what we see here is only the vector root condition of the document   play video starting at  3 14 and follow transcript3 14  of course  the document has all information  for example  the orders of words are  inaudible  model and that s because we assume that the  inaudible  of words will  inaudible   so with this presentation you can really see d1 simply suggests a  inaudible  library  now this is different from another document which might be recommended as a different vector  d2 here  now in this case  the document that covers programming and library  but it doesn t talk about presidential  so what does this remind you  well you can probably guess the topic is likely about program language and the library is software lab library   play video starting at  3 58 and follow transcript3 58  so this shows that by using this vector space reproduction  we can actually capture the differences between topics of documents   play video starting at  4 9 and follow transcript4 09  now you can also imagine there are other vectors  for example  d3 is pointing into that direction  that might be a presidential program  and in fact we can place all the documents in this vector space  and they will be pointing to all kinds of directions  and similarly  we re going to place our query also in this space  as another vector   play video starting at  4 32 and follow transcript4 32  and then we re going to measure the similarity between the query vector and every document vector  so in this case for example  we can easily see d2 seems to be the closest to this query vector  and therefore  d2 will be rendered above others   play video starting at  4 51 and follow transcript4 51  so this is basically the main idea of the vector space model   play video starting at  4 58 and follow transcript4 58  so to be more precise  vector space model is a framework  in this framework  we make the following assumptions  first  we represent a document and query by a term vector   play video starting at  5 18 and follow transcript5 18  so here a term can be any basic concept  for example  a word or a phrase or even n gram of characters  those are just sequence of characters inside a word   play video starting at  5 34 and follow transcript5 34  each term is assumed that will be defined by one dimension  therefore n terms in our vocabulary  we define n dimensional space   play video starting at  5 44 and follow transcript5 44  a query vector would consist of a number of elements  play video starting at  5 49 and follow transcript5 49  corresponding to the weights on different terms   play video starting at  5 56 and follow transcript5 56  each document vector is also similar  it has a number of elements and each value of each element is indicating the weight of the corresponding term  here  you can see  we assume there are n dimensions  therefore  they are n elements  play video starting at  6 15 and follow transcript6 15  each corresponding to the weight on the particular term   play video starting at  6 21 and follow transcript6 21  so the relevance in this case will be assumed to be the similarity between the two vectors   play video starting at  6 29 and follow transcript6 29  therefore  our ranking function is also defined as the similarity between the query vector and document vector   play video starting at  6 37 and follow transcript6 37  now if i ask you to write a program to implement this approach in a search engine   play video starting at  6 44 and follow transcript6 44  you would realize that this was far from clear  we haven t said a lot of things in detail  therefore it s impossible to actually write the program to implement this  that s why i said  this is a framework   play video starting at  6 59 and follow transcript6 59  and this has to be refined in order to actually  play video starting at  7 4 and follow transcript7 04  suggest a particular ranking function that you can implement on a computer   play video starting at  7 10 and follow transcript7 10  so what does this framework not say  well  it actually hasn t said many things that would be required in order to implement this function   play video starting at  7 24 and follow transcript7 24  first  it did not say how we should define or select the basic concepts exactly   play video starting at  7 32 and follow transcript7 32  we clearly assume the concepts are orthogonal  otherwise  there will be redundancy  for example  if two synonyms or somehow distinguish it as two different concepts  then they would be defining two different dimensions and that would clearly cause redundancy here  or all the emphasizing of matching this concept  because it would be as if you match the two dimensions when you actually matched one semantic concept   play video starting at  8 11 and follow transcript8 11  secondly  it did not say how we exactly should place documents and the query in this space  basically that show you some examples of query and document vectors  but where exactly should the vector for a particular document point to   play video starting at  8 29 and follow transcript8 29  so this is equivalent to how to define the term weights  how do you compute the lose element values in those vectors  this is a very important question  because term weight in the query vector indicates the importance of term   play video starting at  8 48 and follow transcript8 48  so depending on how you assign the weight  you might prefer some terms to be matched over others   play video starting at  8 56 and follow transcript8 56  similarly  the total word in the document is also very meaningful  it indicates how well the term characterizes the document  if you got it wrong then you clearly don t represent this document accurately   play video starting at  9 10 and follow transcript9 10  finally  how to define the similarity measure is also not given  so these questions must be addressed before we can have a operational function that we can actually implement using a program language   play video starting at  9 25 and follow transcript9 25  so how do we solve these problems is the main topic of the next lecture   music 
