brief overview of bag of words bag of words  bow   we will start with the basic ml problem in nlp which is document classification  assume you were asked to write an algorithm to detect if an email is a spam or not  an ml approach is to train the computer to create rules using the data we provided  the data is the list of emails and the labels of whether the email is a spam or not  a simple approach would be to use a bag of words  bow   first  we tokenize all the documents into a list of words  then create a dictionary list of all of the words that we see in the articles     then we can use an algorithm to determine the count of each word that would classify an email content as spam or not  as an example logic  if an email has at least two counts of the words  buy  or one count of the word  purchase  and two counts of the word  viagra   then it is a spam  there are quite a few algorithms that can help us create these rules such as logistic regression or random forest  we won t go into detail on the algorithms but you can see detail example of text classification in this post 
provides a high level overview of the meta toolkit and its purpose meta is a modern c   data sciences toolkit featuring    text tokenization  including deep semantic features like parse trees  inverted and forward indexes with compression and various caching strategies  a collection of ranking functions for searching the indexes  topic models  classification algorithms  graph algorithms  language models  crf implementation  pos tagging  shallow parsing   wrappers for liblinear and libsvm  including libsvm dataset parsers   utf8 support for analysis on various languages  multithreaded algorithms  
describes glove method for generating word embeddings  glove  in 2014  there is another approach for generating a word embedding called glove  global vectors for word representation  from pennington et al  it aggregates the global word word co occurrence matrix from a corpus to produce word embeddings  glove s approach is to count up how many words that a word i  context word  appears in a different word j  target word   the goal is to define a context for word i and word j as to whether or not the two words appear in close proximity of n word apart  the encoding vector contains the ratio of the co occurrence probabilities of two words explicitly known as a count based method  in contrast with the skip gram approach  the encoding vector contains the probabilities of their co occurrence that the model predicts known as a prediction based method     the prediction based method gains more supports but the authors of glove argue that the count based approach captures the global statistic and can be more advantageous as it outperforms the learning of word representation on word analogy  word similarity  and named entity recognition tasks     below is the graph of the accuracy of glove vs word2vec approaches using vectors size of 300 with 400 000 vocabulary size and window of size 10  number of surrounding words  on a 6 billion tokens corpus 
