discounted cumulative gain   dcg dcg uses a graded relevance scale of documents from the result set to evaluate the usefulness  or gain  of a document based on its position in the result list  the premise of dcg is that highly relevant documents appearing lower in a search result list should be penalized as the graded relevance value is reduced logarithmically proportional to the position of the result     the dcg accumulated at a particular rank position   displaystyle p  is defined as       displaystyle  mathrm  dcg_ p     sum _ i 1   p   frac  rel_ i    log _ 2  i 1       since result set may vary in size among different queries or systems  to compare performances the normalised version of dcg uses an ideal dcg  to this end  it sorts documents of a result list by relevance  producing an ideal dcg at position p    displaystyle idcg_ p     which normalizes the score       displaystyle  mathrm  ndcg_ p      frac  dcg_ p   idcg p       the ndcg values for all queries can be averaged to obtain a measure of the average performance of a ranking algorithm  note that in a perfect ranking algorithm  the   displaystyle dcg_ p   will be the same as the   displaystyle idcg_ p   producing an ndcg of 1 0  all ndcg calculations are then relative values on the interval 0 0 to 1 0 and so are cross query comparable 
intuition behind idf in other words  the underlying idea of idf is that the more frequently the term appears in the collection  the less informative the term is 
