tarting at  1 58 and follow transcript1 58  a user will typically give a query to the system to express information need  and then  the system would return relevant documents to users  relevant documents refer to those documents that are useful to the user who typed in the query   play video starting at  2 16 and follow transcript2 16  all this task is a phone call that information retrieval   play video starting at  2 21 and follow transcript2 21  but literally information retrieval would broadly include the retrieval of other non textual information as well  for example audio  video  etc  it s worth noting that text retrieval is at the core of information retrieval in the sense that other medias such as video can be retrieved by exploiting the companion text data  so for example  current the image search engines actually match a user s query was the companion text data of the image   play video starting at  2 59 and follow transcript2 59  this problem is also called search problem   play video starting at  3 5 and follow transcript3 05  and the technology is often called the search technology industry   play video starting at  3 11 and follow transcript3 11  if you ever take a course in databases it will be useful to pause the lecture at this point and think about the differences between text retrieval and database retrieval  now these two tasks are similar in many ways   play video starting at  3 29 and follow transcript3 29  but  there are some important differences   play video starting at  3 33 and follow transcript3 33  so  spend a moment to think about the differences between the two  think about the data  and the information managed by a search engine versus those that are managed by a database system   play video starting at  3 47 and follow transcript3 47  think about the different between the queries that you typically specify for database system versus queries that are typed in by users in a search engine   play video starting at  3 59 and follow transcript3 59  and then finally think about the answers   play video starting at  4 2 and follow transcript4 02  what s the difference between the two  okay  so if we think about the information or data managed by the two systems  we will see that in text retrieval  the data is unstructured  it s free text  but in databases  they are structured data where there is a clear defined schema to tell you this column is the names of people and that column is ages  etc   play video starting at  4 31 and follow transcript4 31  the unstructured text is not obvious what are the names of people mentioned in the text   play video starting at  4 40 and follow transcript4 40  because of this difference  we also see that text information tends to be more ambiguous and we talk about that in the processing chapter  whereas in databases  but they don t tend to have where to find the semantics   play video starting at  4 58 and follow transcript4 58  the results important difference in the queries  and this is partly due to the difference in the information or data   play video starting at  5 7 and follow transcript5 07  so test queries tend to be ambiguous  whereas in their research  the queries are typically well defined  think about a sql query that would clearly specify what records to be returned  so it has very well defined semantics   play video starting at  5 27 and follow transcript5 27  keyword queries or electronic queries tend to be incomplete  also in that it doesn t really specify what documents should be retrieved  whereas complete specification for what should be returned   play video starting at  5 47 and follow transcript5 47  and because of these differences  the answers would be also different  being the case of text retrieval  we re looking for it rather than the documents   play video starting at  5 58 and follow transcript5 58  in the database search  we are retrieving records or match records with the sequel query more precisely   play video starting at  6 9 and follow transcript6 09  now in the case of text retrieval  what should be the right answers to the query is not very well specified  as we just discussed   play video starting at  6 21 and follow transcript6 21  so it s unclear what should be the right answers to a query  and this has very important consequences  and that is  textual retrieval is an empirically defined problem   play video starting at  6 38 and follow transcript6 38  so this is a problem because if it s empirically defined  then we can not mathematically prove one method is better than another method   play video starting at  6 52 and follow transcript6 52  that also means we must rely on empirical evaluation involving users to know which method works better   play video starting at  7 2 and follow transcript7 02  and that s why we have  you need more than one lectures to cover the issue of evaluation  because this is very important topic for sir jennings   play video starting at  7 13 and follow transcript7 13  without knowing how to evaluate heroism properly  there s no way to tell whether we have got the better or whether one system is better than another   play video starting at  7 28 and follow transcript7 28  so now let s look at the problem in a formal way   play video starting at  7 32 and follow transcript7 32  so  this slide shows a formal formulation of the text retrieval problem   play video starting at  7 37 and follow transcript7 37  first  we have our vocabulary set  which is just a set of words in a language   play video starting at  7 44 and follow transcript7 44  now here  we are considering only one language  but in reality  on the web  there might be multiple natural languages  we have texts that are in all kinds of languages   play video starting at  7 57 and follow transcript7 57  but here for simplicity  we just assume that is one kind of language  as the techniques used for retrieving data from multiple languages are more or less similar to the techniques used for retrieving documents in one end  which although there is important difference  the principle methods are very similar   play video starting at  8 21 and follow transcript8 21  next  we have the query  which is a sequence of words   play video starting at  8 26 and follow transcript8 26  and so here  you can see  play video starting at  8 31 and follow transcript8 31  the query is defined as a sequence of words  each q sub i is a word in the vocabulary   play video starting at  8 42 and follow transcript8 42  a document is defined in the same way  so it s also a sequence of words  and here  d sub ij is also a word in the vocabulary   play video starting at  8 52 and follow transcript8 52  now typically  the documents are much longer than queries   play video starting at  8 57 and follow transcript8 57  but there are also cases where the documents may be very short   play video starting at  9 4 and follow transcript9 04  so you can think about what might be a example of that case   play video starting at  9 9 and follow transcript9 09  i hope you can think of twitter search  tweets are very short   play video starting at  9 16 and follow transcript9 16  but in general  documents are longer than the queries   play video starting at  9 22 and follow transcript9 22  now  then we have a collection of documents  and this collection can be very large  so think about the web  it could be very large   play video starting at  9 36 and follow transcript9 36  and then the goal of text retrieval is you ll find the set of relevant in the documents  which we denote by r  q   because it depends on the query  and this in general  a subset of all the documents in the collection   play video starting at  9 52 and follow transcript9 52  unfortunately  this set of relevant documents is generally unknown  and user dependent in the sense that  for the same query typed in by different users  they expect the relevant documents may be different   play video starting at  10 9 and follow transcript10 09  the query given to us by the user is only a hint on which document should be in this set   play video starting at  10 17 and follow transcript10 17  and indeed  the user is generally unable to specify what exactly should be in this set  especially in the case of web search  where the connection s so large  the user doesn t have complete knowledge about the whole production   play video starting at  10 34 and follow transcript10 34  so the best search system can do is to compute an approximation of this relevant document set  so we denote it by r  q   so formerly  we can see the task is to compute this r  q  approximation of the relevant documents  so how can we do that  now imagine if you are now asked to write a program to do this   play video starting at  11 8 and follow transcript11 08  what would you do  now think for a moment  right  so these are your input  the query  the documents   play video starting at  11 20 and follow transcript11 20  and then you are to compute the answers to this query  which is a set of documents that would be useful to the user   play video starting at  11 29 and follow transcript11 29  so  how would you solve the problem  now in general  there are two strategies that we can use   play video starting at  11 39 and follow transcript11 39  the first strategy is we do a document selection  and that is  we re going to have a binary classification function  or binary classifier   play video starting at  11 49 and follow transcript11 49  that s a function that would take a document and query as input  and then give a zero or one as output to indicate whether this document is relevant to the query or not   play video starting at  12 2 and follow transcript12 02  so in this case  you can see the document   play video starting at  12 8 and follow transcript12 08  the relevant document is set  is defined as follows  it basically  all the documents that have a value of 1 by this function   play video starting at  12 25 and follow transcript12 25  so in this case  you can see the system must have decide if the document is relevant or not  basically  it has to say whether it s one or zero  and this is called absolute relevance  basically  it needs to know exactly whether it s going to be useful to the user   play video starting at  12 41 and follow transcript12 41  alternatively  there s another strategy called document ranking   play video starting at  12 46 and follow transcript12 46  now in this case  the system is not going to make a call whether a document is random or not  but rather the system is going to use a real value function  f here   play video starting at  12 58 and follow transcript12 58  that would simply give us a value that would indicate which document is more likely relevant   play video starting at  13 5 and follow transcript13 05  so it s not going to make a call whether this document is relevant or not  but rather it would say which document is more likely relevant  so this function then can be used to random documents  and then we re going to let the user decide where to stop  when the user looks at the document  so we have a threshold theta here to determine what documents should be in this approximation set  and we re going to assume that all the documents that are ranked above the threshold are in this set  because in effect  these are the documents that we deliver to the user  and theta is a cutoff determined by the user   play video starting at  13 56 and follow transcript13 56  so here we ve got some collaboration from the user in some sense  because we don t really make a cutoff  and the user kind of helped the system make a cutoff   play video starting at  14 8 and follow transcript14 08  so in this case  the system only needs to decide if one document is more likely relevant than another  and that is  it only needs to determine relative relevance   play video starting at  14 19 and follow transcript14 19  as opposed to absolute relevance   play video starting at  14 22 and follow transcript14 22  now you can probably already sense that relative relevance would be easier to determine than absolute relevance  because in the first case  we have to say exactly whether a document is relevant or not   play video starting at  14 37 and follow transcript14 37  and it turns out that ranking is indeed generally preferred to document selection   play video starting at  14 46 and follow transcript14 46  so let s look at these two strategies in more detail  so this picture shows how it works  so on the left side  we see these documents  and we use the pluses to indicate the relevant documents  so we can see the true relevant documents here consists this set of true relevant documents  consists of these process  these documents   play video starting at  15 17 and follow transcript15 17  and with the document selection function  we re going to basically classify them into two groups  relevant documents  and non relevant ones  of course  the classified will not be perfect so it will make mistakes  so here we can see  in the approximation of the relevant documents  we have got some number in the documents   play video starting at  15 43 and follow transcript15 43  and similarly  there is a relevant document that s misclassified as non relevant  in the case of document ranking  we can see the system seems like  simply ranks all the documents in the descending order of the scores  and then  we re going to let the user stop wherever the user wants to stop  if the user wants to examine more documents  then the user will scroll down some more and then stop  inaudible   but if the user only wants to read a few random documents  the user might stop at the top position  so in this case  the user stops at d4  so in fact  we have delivered these four documents to our user   play video starting at  16 33 and follow transcript16 33  so as i said ranking is generally preferred  and one of the reasons is because the classifier in the case of document selection is unlikely accurate  why  because the only clue is usually the query  but the query may not be accurate in the sense that it could be overly constrained   play video starting at  16 57 and follow transcript16 57  for example  you might expect relevant documents to talk about all these  play video starting at  17 4 and follow transcript17 04  topics by using specific vocabulary  and as a result  you might match no relevant documents  because in the collection  no others have discussed the topic using these vocabularies  right  so in this case  we ll see there is this problem of  play video starting at  17 25 and follow transcript17 25  no relevant documents to return in the case of over constrained query   play video starting at  17 33 and follow transcript17 33  on the other hand  if the query is under constrained  for example  if the query does not have sufficient descriptive words to find the random documents  you may actually end up having of over delivery  and this when you thought these words my be sufficient to help you find the right documents  but  it turns out they are not sufficient and there are many distractions  documents using similar words  and so  this is a case of over delivery   play video starting at  18 8 and follow transcript18 08  unfortunately  it s very hard to find the right position between these two extremes   play video starting at  18 15 and follow transcript18 15  why  because whether users looking for the information in general the user does not have a good knowledge about the information to be found  and in that case  the user does not have a good knowledge about what  play video starting at  18 30 and follow transcript18 30  vocabularies will be used in those relevent documents  so it s very hard for a user to pre specify the right level of constraints   play video starting at  18 44 and follow transcript18 44  even if the classifier is accurate  we also still want to rend these relevant documents  because they are generally not equally relevant   play video starting at  18 56 and follow transcript18 56  relevance is often a matter of degree   play video starting at  18 59 and follow transcript18 59  so we must prioritize these documents for a user to examine   play video starting at  19 6 and follow transcript19 06  and note that this prioritization is very important  play video starting at  19 12 and follow transcript19 12  because a user cannot digest all the content the user generally would have to look at each document sequentially   play video starting at  19 21 and follow transcript19 21  and therefore  it would make sense to users with the most relevant documents  and that s what ranking is doing  so for these reasons  ranking is generally preferred   play video starting at  19 36 and follow transcript19 36  now this preference also has a theoretical justification and this is given by the probability ranking principle   play video starting at  19 44 and follow transcript19 44  in the end of this lecture  there is reference for this   play video starting at  19 49 and follow transcript19 49  this principle says  returning a ranked list of documents in descending order of probability that a document is relevant to the query is the optimal strategy under the following two assumptions   play video starting at  20 2 and follow transcript20 02  first  the utility of a document  to a user  is independent of the utility of any other document   play video starting at  20 10 and follow transcript20 10  second  a user would be assumed to browse the results sequentially  now it s easy to understand why these assumptions are needed in order to justify site for the ranking strategy  because if the documents are independent  then we can evaluate the utility of each document that s separate   play video starting at  20 36 and follow transcript20 36  and this would allow the computer score for each document independently  and then  we are going to rank these documents based on the scrolls   play video starting at  20 45 and follow transcript20 45  the second assumption is to say that the user would indeed follow the rank list  if the user is not going to follow the ranked list  is not going to examine the documents sequentially  then obviously the ordering would not be optimal   play video starting at  21  and follow transcript21 00  so under these two assumptions  we can theoretically justify the ranking strategy is  in fact  the best that you could do  now  i ve put one question here  do these two assumptions hold   play video starting at  21 18 and follow transcript21 18  i suggest you to pause the lecture  for a moment  to think about this   play video starting at  21 27 and follow transcript21 27  now  can you think of some examples that would suggest these assumptions aren t necessarily true   play video starting at  21 44 and follow transcript21 44  now  if you think for a moment  you may realize none of the assumptions is actually true   play video starting at  21 53 and follow transcript21 53  for example  in the case of independence assumption we might have documents that have similar or exactly the same content  if we look at each of them alone  each is relevant   play video starting at  22 7 and follow transcript22 07  but if the user has already seen one of them  we can assume it s generally not very useful for the user to see another similar or duplicated one   play video starting at  22 19 and follow transcript22 19  so clearly the utility on the document that is dependent on other documents that the user has seen   play video starting at  22 27 and follow transcript22 27  in some other cases you might see a scenario where one document that may not be useful to the user  but when three particular documents are put together  they provide answers to the user s question   play video starting at  22 42 and follow transcript22 42  so this is a collective relevance and that also suggests that the value of the document might depend on other documents   play video starting at  22 53 and follow transcript22 53  sequential browsing generally would make sense if you have a ranked list there   play video starting at  22 59 and follow transcript22 59  but even if you have a rank list  there is evidence showing that users don t always just go strictly sequentially through the entire list  they sometimes will look at the bottom for example  or skip some  and if you think about the more complicated interfaces that we could possibly use like two dimensional in the phase  where you can put that additional information on the screen then sequential browsing is a very restricted assumption   play video starting at  23 32 and follow transcript23 32  so the point here is that  play video starting at  23 35 and follow transcript23 35  none of these assumptions is really true but less than that   play video starting at  23 41 and follow transcript23 41  but probability ranking principle establishes some solid foundation for  play video starting at  23 46 and follow transcript23 46  ranking as a primary pattern for search engines  and this has actually been the basis for a lot of research work in information retrieval  and many hours have been designed based on this assumption   play video starting at  24 1 and follow transcript24 01  despite that the assumptions aren t necessarily true  and we can address this problem by doing post processing of a ranked list  for example  to remove redundancy   play video starting at  24 20 and follow transcript24 20  so to summarize this lecture  the main points that you can take away are the following  first  text retrieval is an empirically defined problem  and that means which algorithm is better must be judged by the users  second  document ranking is generally preferred  and this will help users prioritize examination of search results   play video starting at  24 47 and follow transcript24 47  and this is also to bypass the difficulty in determining absolute relevance because we can get some help from users in determining where to make the cut off  it s more flexible   play video starting at  25 1 and follow transcript25 01  so  this further suggests that the main technical challenge in designing a search engine is the design effective ranking function   play video starting at  25 10 and follow transcript25 10  in other words  we need to define what is the value of this function f on the query and document pair   play video starting at  25 21 and follow transcript25 21  how we design such a function is the main topic in the following lectures   play video starting at  25 29 and follow transcript25 29  there are two suggested additional readings  the first is the classical paper on the probability ranking principle   play video starting at  25 37 and follow transcript25 37  the second one is a must read for anyone doing research on information retrieval  it s a classic ir book  which has excellent coverage of the main research and results in early days up to the time when the book was written  chapter six of this book has an in depth discussion of the probability ranking principle and probably for retrieval models in general   music 
text retrieval problem  music  this lecture is about the text retrieval problem   play video starting at   12 and follow transcript0 12  this picture shows our overall plan for lectures   play video starting at   16 and follow transcript0 16  in the last lecture  we talked about the high level strategies for text access  we talked about push versus pull   play video starting at   25 and follow transcript0 25  such engines are the main tools for supporting the pull mode  starting from this lecture  we re going to talk about the how search engines work in detail   play video starting at   38 and follow transcript0 38  so first it s about the text retrieval problem   play video starting at   42 and follow transcript0 42  we re going to talk about the three things in this lecture  first  we define text retrieval  second we re going to make a comparison between text retrieval and the related task database retrieval   play video starting at   58 and follow transcript0 58  finally  we re going to talk about the document selection versus document ranking as two strategies for responding to a user s query   play video starting at  1 9 and follow transcript1 09  so what is text retrieval   play video starting at  1 12 and follow transcript1 12  it should be a task that s familiar for the most of us because we re using web search engines all the time   play video starting at  1 19 and follow transcript1 19  so text retrieval is basically a task where the system would respond to a user s query with relevant documents  basically  it s for supporting a query  play video starting at  1 32 and follow transcript1 32  as one way to implement the poll mode of information access   play video starting at  1 39 and follow transcript1 39  so the situation is the following  you have a collection of text retrieval documents  these documents could be all the webpages on the web  or all the literature articles in the digital library  or maybe all the text files in your computer   play video starting at  1 58 and follow transcript1 58  a user will typically give a query to the system to express information need  and then  the system would return relevant documents to users  relevant documents refer to those documents that are useful to the user who typed in the query   play video starting at  2 16 and follow transcript2 16  all this task is a phone call that information retrieval   play video starting at  2 21 and follow transcript2 21  but literally information retrieval would broadly include the retrieval of other non textual information as well  for example audio  video  etc  it s worth noting that text retrieval is at the core of information retrieval in the sense that other medias such as video can be retrieved by exploiting the companion text data  so for example  current the image search engines actually match a user s query was the companion text data of the image   play video starting at  2 59 and follow transcript2 59  this problem is also called search problem   play video starting at  3 5 and follow transcript3 05  and the technology is often called the search technology industry   play video starting at  3 11 and follow transcript3 11  if you ever take a course in databases it will be useful to pause the lecture at this point and think about the differences between text retrieval and database retrieval  now these two tasks are similar in many ways   play video starting at  3 29 and follow transcript3 29  but  there are some important differences   play video starting at  3 33 and follow transcript3 33  so  spend a moment to think about the differences between the two  think about the data  and the information managed by a search engine versus those that are managed by a database system   play video starting at  3 47 and follow transcript3 47  think about the different between the queries that you typically specify for database system versus queries that are typed in by users in a search engine   play video starting at  3 59 and follow transcript3 59  and then finally think about the answers   play video starting at  4 2 and follow transcript4 02  what s the difference between the two  okay  so if we think about the information or data managed by the two systems  we will see that in text retrieval  the data is unstructured  it s free text  but in databases  they are structured data where there is a clear defined schema to tell you this column is the names of people and that column is ages  etc   play video starting at  4 31 and follow transcript4 31  the unstructured text is not obvious what are the names of people mentioned in the text   play video starting at  4 40 and follow transcript4 40  because of this difference  we also see that text information tends to be more ambiguous and we talk about that in the processing chapter  whereas in databases  but they don t tend to have where to find the semantics   play video starting at  4 58 and follow transcript4 58  the results important difference in the queries  and this is partly due to the difference in the information or data   play video starting at  5 7 and follow transcript5 07  so test queries tend to be ambiguous  whereas in their research  the queries are typically well defined  think about a sql query that would clearly specify what records to be returned  so it has very well defined semantics   play video starting at  5 27 and follow transcript5 27  keyword queries or electronic queries tend to be incomplete  also in that it doesn t really specify what documents should be retrieved  whereas complete specification for what should be returned   play video starting at  5 47 and follow transcript5 47  and because of these differences  the answers would be also different  being the case of text retrieval  we re looking for it rather than the documents   play video starting at  5 58 and follow transcript5 58  in the database search  we are retrieving records or match records with the sequel query more precisely   play video starting at  6 9 and follow transcript6 09  now in the case of text retrieval  what should be the right answers to the query is not very well specified  as we just discussed   play video starting at  6 21 and follow transcript6 21  so it s unclear what should be the right answers to a query  and this has very important consequences  and that is  textual retrieval is an empirically defined problem   play video starting at  6 38 and follow transcript6 38  so this is a problem because if it s empirically defined  then we can not mathematically prove one method is better than another method   play video starting at  6 52 and follow transcript6 52  that also means we must rely on empirical evaluation involving users to know which method works better   play video starting at  7 2 and follow transcript7 02  and that s why we have  you need more than one lectures to cover the issue of evaluation  because this is very important topic for sir jennings   play video starting at  7 13 and follow transcript7 13  without knowing how to evaluate heroism properly  there s no way to tell whether we have got the better or whether one system is better than another   play video starting at  7 28 and follow transcript7 28  so now let s look at the problem in a formal way   play video starting at  7 32 and follow transcript7 32  so  this slide shows a formal formulation of the text retrieval problem   play video starting at  7 37 and follow transcript7 37  first  we have our vocabulary set  which is just a set of words in a language   play video starting at  7 44 and follow transcript7 44  now here  we are considering only one language  but in reality  on the web  there might be multiple natural languages  we have texts that are in all kinds of languages   play video starting at  7 57 and follow transcript7 57  but here for simplicity  we just assume that is one kind of language  as the techniques used for retrieving data from multiple languages are more or less similar to the techniques used for retrieving documents in one end  which although there is important difference  the principle methods are very similar   play video starting at  8 21 and follow transcript8 21  next  we have the query  which is a sequence of words   play video starting at  8 26 and follow transcript8 26  and so here  you can see  play video starting at  8 31 and follow transcript8 31  the query is defined as a sequence of words  each q sub i is a word in the vocabulary   play video starting at  8 42 and follow transcript8 42  a document is defined in the same way  so it s also a sequence of words  and here  d sub ij is also a word in the vocabulary   play video starting at  8 52 and follow transcript8 52  now typically  the documents are much longer than queries   play video starting at  8 57 and follow transcript8 57  but there are also cases where the documents may be very short   play video starting at  9 4 and follow transcript9 04  so you can think about what might be a example of that case   play video starting at  9 9 and follow transcript9 09  i hope you can think of twitter search  tweets are very short   play video starting at  9 16 and follow transcript9 16  but in general  documents are longer than the queries   play video starting at  9 22 and follow transcript9 22  now  then we have a collection of documents  and this collection can be very large  so think about the web  it could be very large   play video starting at  9 36 and follow transcript9 36  and then the goal of text retrieval is you ll find the set of relevant in the documents  which we denote by r  q   because it depends on the query  and this in general  a subset of all the documents in the collection   play video starting at  9 52 and follow transcript9 52  unfortunately  this set of relevant documents is generally unknown  and user dependent in the sense that  for the same query typed in by different users  they expect the relevant documents may be different   play video starting at  10 9 and follow transcript10 09  the query given to us by the user is only a hint on which document should be in this set   play video starting at  10 17 and follow transcript10 17  and indeed  the user is generally unable to specify what exactly should be in this set  especially in the case of web search  where the connection s so large  the user doesn t have complete knowledge about the whole production   play video starting at  10 34 and follow transcript10 34  so the best search system can do is to compute an approximation of this relevant document set  so we denote it by r  q   so formerly  we can see the task is to compute this r  q  approximation of the relevant documents  so how can we do that  now imagine if you are now asked to write a program to do this   play video starting at  11 8 and follow transcript11 08  what would you do  now think for a moment  right  so these are your input  the query  the documents   play video starting at  11 20 and follow transcript11 20  and then you are to compute the answers to this query  which is a set of documents that would be useful to the user   play video starting at  11 29 and follow transcript11 29  so  how would you solve the problem  now in general  there are two strategies that we can use   play video starting at  11 39 and follow transcript11 39  the first strategy is we do a document selection  and that is  we re going to have a binary classification function  or binary classifier   play video starting at  11 49 and follow transcript11 49  that s a function that would take a document and query as input  and then give a zero or one as output to indicate whether this document is relevant to the query or not   play video starting at  12 2 and follow transcript12 02  so in this case  you can see the document   play video starting at  12 8 and follow transcript12 08  the relevant document is set  is defined as follows  it basically  all the documents that have a value of 1 by this function   play video starting at  12 25 and follow transcript12 25  so in this case  you can see the system must have decide if the document is relevant or not  basically  it has to say whether it s one or zero  and this is called absolute relevance  basically  it needs to know exactly whether it s going to be useful to the user   play video starting at  12 41 and follow transcript12 41  alternatively  there s another strategy called document ranking   play video starting at  12 46 and follow transcript12 46  now in this case  the system is not going to make a call whether a document is random or not  but rather the system is going to use a real value function  f here   play video starting at  12 58 and follow transcript12 58  that would simply give us a value that would indicate which document is more likely relevant   play video starting at  13 5 and follow transcript13 05  so it s not going to make a call whether this document is relevant or not  but rather it would say which document is more likely relevant  so this function then can be used to random documents  and then we re going to let the user decide where to stop  when the user looks at the document  so we have a threshold theta here to determine what documents should be in this approximation set  and we re going to assume that all the documents that are ranked above the threshold are in this set  because in effect  these are the documents that we deliver to the user  and theta is a cutoff determined by the user   play video starting at  13 56 and follow transcript13 56  so here we ve got some collaboration from the user in some sense  because we don t really make a cutoff  and the user kind of helped the system make a cutoff   play video starting at  14 8 and follow transcript14 08  so in this case  the system only needs to decide if one document is more likely relevant than another  and that is  it only needs to determine relative relevance   play video starting at  14 19 and follow transcript14 19  as opposed to absolute relevance   play video starting at  14 22 and follow transcript14 22  now you can probably already sense that relative relevance would be easier to determine than absolute relevance  because in the first case  we have to say exactly whether a document is relevant or not   play video starting at  14 37 and follow transcript14 37  and it turns out that ranking is indeed generally preferred to document selection   play video starting at  14 46 and follow transcript14 46  so let s look at these two strategies in more detail  so this picture shows how it works  so on the left side  we see these documents  and we use the pluses to indicate the relevant documents  so we can see the true relevant documents here consists this set of true relevant documents  consists of these process  these documents   play video starting at  15 17 and follow transcript15 17  and with the document selection function  we re going to basically classify them into two groups  relevant documents  and non relevant ones  of course  the classified will not be perfect so it will make mistakes  so here we can see  in the approximation of the relevant documents  we have got some number in the documents   play video starting at  15 43 and follow transcript15 43  and similarly  there is a relevant document that s misclassified as non relevant  in the case of document ranking  we can see the system seems like  simply ranks all the documents in the descending order of the scores  and then  we re going to let the user stop wherever the user wants to stop  if the user wants to examine more documents  then the user will scroll down some more and then stop  inaudible   but if the user only wants to read a few random documents  the user might stop at the top position  so in this case  the user stops at d4  so in fact  we have delivered these four documents to our user   play video starting at  16 33 and follow transcript16 33  so as i said ranking is generally preferred  and one of the reasons is because the classifier in the case of document selection is unlikely accurate  why  because the only clue is usually the query  but the query may not be accurate in the sense that it could be overly constrained   play video starting at  16 57 and follow transcript16 57  for example  you might expect relevant documents to talk about all these  play video starting at  17 4 and follow transcript17 04  topics by using specific vocabulary  and as a result  you might match no relevant documents  because in the collection  no others have discussed the topic using these vocabularies  right  so in this case  we ll see there is this problem of  play video starting at  17 25 and follow transcript17 25  no relevant documents to return in the case of over constrained query   play video starting at  17 33 and follow transcript17 33  on the other hand  if the query is under constrained  for example  if the query does not have sufficient descriptive words to find the random documents  you may actually end up having of over delivery  and this when you thought these words my be sufficient to help you find the right documents  but  it turns out they are not sufficient and there are many distractions  documents using similar words  and so  this is a case of over delivery   play video starting at  18 8 and follow transcript18 08  unfortunately  it s very hard to find the right position between these two extremes   play video starting at  18 15 and follow transcript18 15  why  because whether users looking for the information in general the user does not have a good knowledge about the information to be found  and in that case  the user does not have a good knowledge about what  play video starting at  18 30 and follow transcript18 30  vocabularies will be used in those relevent documents  so it s very hard for a user to pre specify the right level of constraints   play video starting at  18 44 and follow transcript18 44  even if the classifier is accurate  we also still want to rend these relevant documents  because they are generally not equally relevant   play video starting at  18 56 and follow transcript18 56  relevance is often a matter of degree   play video starting at  18 59 and follow transcript18 59  so we must prioritize these documents for a user to examine   play video starting at  19 6 and follow transcript19 06  and note that this prioritization is very important  play video starting at  19 12 and follow transcript19 12  because a user cannot digest all the content the user generally would have to look at each document sequentially   play video starting at  19 21 and follow transcript19 21  and therefore  it would make sense to users with the most relevant documents  and that s what ranking is doing  so for these reasons  ranking is generally preferred   play video starting at  19 36 and follow transcript19 36  now this preference also has a theoretical justification and this is given by the probability ranking principle   play video starting at  19 44 and follow transcript19 44  in the end of this lecture  there is reference for this   play video starting at  19 49 and follow transcript19 49  this principle says  returning a ranked list of documents in descending order of probability that a document is relevant to the query is the optimal strategy under the following two assumptions   play video starting at  20 2 and follow transcript20 02  first  the utility of a document  to a user  is independent of the utility of any other document   play video starting at  20 10 and follow transcript20 10  second  a user would be assumed to browse the results sequentially  now it s easy to understand why these assumptions are needed in order to justify site for the ranking strategy  because if the documents are independent  then we can evaluate the utility of each document that s separate   play video starting at  20 36 and follow transcript20 36  and this would allow the computer score for each document independently  and then  we are going to rank these documents based on the scrolls   play video starting 