overview of text retrieval methods  sound  this lecture is a overview of text retrieval methods   play video starting at   13 and follow transcript0 13  in the previous lecture  we introduced the problem of text retrieval  we explained that the main problem is the design of ranking function to rank documents for a query  in this lecture  we will give an overview of different ways of designing this ranking function   play video starting at   33 and follow transcript0 33  so the problem is the following  we have a query that has a sequence of words and the document that s also a sequence of words  and we hope to define a function f  play video starting at   45 and follow transcript0 45  that can compute a score based on the query and document  so the main challenge you hear is with design a good ranking function that can rank all the relevant documents on top of all the non relevant ones  clearly  this means our function must be able to measure the likelihood that a document d is relevant to a query q  that also means we have to have some way to define relevance  in particular  in order to implement the program to do that  we have to have a computational definition of relevance  and we achieve this goal by designing a retrieval model  which gives us a formalization of relevance   play video starting at  1 32 and follow transcript1 32  now  over many decades  researchers have designed many different kinds of retrieval models  and they fall into different categories   play video starting at  1 42 and follow transcript1 42  first  one family of the models are based on the similarity idea   play video starting at  1 50 and follow transcript1 50  basically  we assume that if a document is more similar to the query than another document is  then we will say the first document is more relevant than the second one  so in this case  the ranking function is defined as the similarity between the query and the document  one well known example in this case is vector space model  which we will cover more in detail later in the lecture   play video starting at  2 20 and follow transcript2 20  a second kind of models are called probabilistic models  in this family of models  we follow a very different strategy  where we assume that queries and documents are all observations from random variables   play video starting at  2 36 and follow transcript2 36  and we assume there is a binary random variable called r here  play video starting at  2 42 and follow transcript2 42  to indicate whether a document is relevant to a query   play video starting at  2 46 and follow transcript2 46  we then define the score of document with respect to a query as a probability that this random variable r is equal to 1  given a particular document query  there are different cases of such a general idea  one is classic probabilistic model  another is language model  yet another is divergence from randomness model   play video starting at  3 12 and follow transcript3 12  in a later lecture  we will talk more about one case  which is language model  a third kind of model are based on probabilistic inference  so here the idea is to associate uncertainty to inference rules  and we can then quantify the probability that we can show that the query follows from the document   play video starting at  3 37 and follow transcript3 37  finally  there is also a family of models that are using axiomatic thinking  here  an idea is to define a set of constraints that we hope a good retrieval function to satisfy   play video starting at  3 55 and follow transcript3 55  so in this case  the problem is to seek a good ranking function that can satisfy all the desired constraints   play video starting at  4 5 and follow transcript4 05  interestingly  although these different models are based on different thinking  in the end  the retrieval function tends to be very similar  and these functions tend to also involve similar variables  so now let s take a look at the common form of a state of the art retrieval model and to examine some of the common ideas used in all these models   play video starting at  4 33 and follow transcript4 33  first  these models are all based on the assumption of using bag of words to represent text  and we explained this in the natural language processing lecture  bag of words representation remains the main representation used in all the search engines   play video starting at  4 53 and follow transcript4 53  so with this assumption  the score of a query  like a presidential campaign news with respect to a document of d here  would be based on scores computed based on each individual word   play video starting at  5 9 and follow transcript5 09  and that means the score would depend on the score of each word  such as presidential  campaign  and news  here  we can see there are three different components  each corresponding to how well the document matches each of the query words   play video starting at  5 31 and follow transcript5 31  inside of these functions  we see a number of heuristics used   play video starting at  5 38 and follow transcript5 38  so for example  one factor that affects the function d here is how many times does the word presidential occur in the document  this is called a term frequency  or tf   play video starting at  5 51 and follow transcript5 51  we might also denote as c of presidential and d  in general  if the word occurs more frequently in the document  then the value of this function would be larger  another factor is  how long is the document  and this is to use the document length for scoring  in general  if a term occurs in a long document many times  it s not as significant as if it occurred the same number of times in a short document  because in a long document  any term is expected to occur more frequently   play video starting at  6 38 and follow transcript6 38  finally  there is this factor called document frequency  that is  we also want to look at how often presidential occurs in the entire collection  and we call this document frequency  or df of presidential  and in some other models  we might also use a probability to characterize this information   play video starting at  7 5 and follow transcript7 05  so here  i show the probability of presidential in the collection   play video starting at  7 10 and follow transcript7 10  so all these are trying to characterize the popularity of the term in the collection  in general  matching a rare term in the collection is contributing more to the overall score than matching up common term   play video starting at  7 25 and follow transcript7 25  so this captures some of the main ideas used in pretty much older state of the art original models   play video starting at  7 34 and follow transcript7 34  so now  a natural question is  which model works the best   play video starting at  7 39 and follow transcript7 39  now it turns out that many models work equally well  so here are a list of the four major models that are generally regarded as a state of the art original models  pivoted length normalization  bm25  query likelihood  pl2  when optimized  these models tend to perform similarly  and this was discussed in detail in this reference at the end of this lecture  among all these  bm25 is probably the most popular  it s most likely that this has been used in virtually all the search engines  and you will also often see this method discussed in research papers   play video starting at  8 22 and follow transcript8 22  and we ll talk more about this method later in some other lectures   play video starting at  8 30 and follow transcript8 30  so  to summarize  the main points made in this lecture are first the design of a good ranking function pre requires a computational definition of relevance  and we achieve this goal by designing appropriate retrieval model   play video starting at  8 47 and follow transcript8 47  second  many models are equally effective  but we don t have a single winner yet  researchers are still active and working on this problem  trying to find a truly optimal retrieval model   play video starting at  9  and follow transcript9 00  finally  the state of the art ranking functions tend to rely on the following ideas  first  bag of words representation  second  tf and document frequency of words  such information is used in the weighting function to determine the overall contribution of matching a word and document length  these are often combined in interesting ways  and we ll discuss how exactly they are combined to rank documents in the lectures later   play video starting at  9 36 and follow transcript9 36  there are two suggested additional readings if you have time   play video starting at  9 41 and follow transcript9 41  the first is a paper where you can find the detailed discussion and comparison of multiple state of the art models   play video starting at  9 49 and follow transcript9 49  the second is a book with a chapter that gives a broad review of different retrieval models   music 
vector space model  sound  this lecture is about the vector space retrieval model  we re going to give an introduction to its basic idea   play video starting at   18 and follow transcript0 18  in the last lecture  we talked about the different ways of designing a retrieval model  which would give us a different arranging function   play video starting at   30 and follow transcript0 30  in this lecture  we re going to talk about a specific way of designing a ramping function called a vector space retrieval model   play video starting at   37 and follow transcript0 37  and we re going to give a brief introduction to the basic idea   play video starting at   44 and follow transcript0 44  vector space model is a special case of similarity based models as we discussed before  which means we assume relevance is roughly similarity  between the document and the query   play video starting at  1 2 and follow transcript1 02  now whether is this assumption is true is actually a question  but in order to solve the search problem  we have to convert the vague notion of relevance into a more precise definition that can be implemented with the program analogy  so in this process  we have to make a number of assumptions  this is the first assumption that we make here  basically  we assume that if a document is more similar to a query than another document  then the first document will be assumed it will be more relevant than the second one  and this is the basis for ranking documents in this approach   play video starting at  1 46 and follow transcript1 46  again  it s questionable whether this is really the best definition for randoms  as we will see later there are other ways to model randoms   play video starting at  1 58 and follow transcript1 58  the basic idea of vectors for base retrieval model is actually very easy to understand  imagine a high dimensional space where each dimension corresponds to a term   play video starting at  2 11 and follow transcript2 11  so here i issue a three dimensional space with three words  programming  library and presidential  so each term here defines one dimension   play video starting at  2 24 and follow transcript2 24  now we can consider vectors in this  three dimensional space  and we re going to assume that all our documents and the query will be placed in this vector space  so for example  on document might be represented by this vector  d1  now this means this document probably covers library and presidential  but it doesn t really talk about programming  what does this mean in terms of representation of document  that just means we re going to look at our document from the perspective of this vector  we re going to ignore everything else  basically  what we see here is only the vector root condition of the document   play video starting at  3 14 and follow transcript3 14  of course  the document has all information  for example  the orders of words are  inaudible  model and that s because we assume that the  inaudible  of words will  inaudible   so with this presentation you can really see d1 simply suggests a  inaudible  library  now this is different from another document which might be recommended as a different vector  d2 here  now in this case  the document that covers programming and library  but it doesn t talk about presidential  so what does this remind you  well you can probably guess the topic is likely about program language and the library is software lab library   play video starting at  3 58 and follow transcript3 58  so this shows that by using this vector space reproduction  we can actually capture the differences between topics of documents   play video starting at  4 9 and follow transcript4 09  now you can also imagine there are other vectors  for example  d3 is pointing into that direction  that might be a presidential program  and in fact we can place all the documents in this vector space  and they will be pointing to all kinds of directions  and similarly  we re going to place our query also in this space  as another vector   play video starting at  4 32 and follow transcript4 32  and then we re going to measure the similarity between the query vector and every document vector  so in this case for example  we can easily see d2 seems to be the closest to this query vector  and therefore  d2 will be rendered above others   play video starting at  4 51 and follow transcript4 51  so this is basically the main idea of the vector space model   play video starting at  4 58 and follow transcript4 58  so to be more precise  vector space model is a framework  in this framework  we make the following assumptions  first  we represent a document and query by a term vector   play video starting at  5 18 and follow transcript5 18  so here a term can be any basic concept  for example  a word or a phrase or even n gram of characters  those are just sequence of characters inside a word   play video starting at  5 34 and follow transcript5 34  each term is assumed that will be defined by one dimension  therefore n terms in our vocabulary  we define n dimensional space   play video starting at  5 44 and follow transcript5 44  a query vector would consist of a number of elements  play video starting at  5 49 and follow transcript5 49  corresponding to the weights on different terms   play video starting at  5 56 and follow transcript5 56  each document vector is also similar  it has a number of elements and each value of each element is indicating the weight of the corresponding term  here  you can see  we assume there are n dimensions  therefore  they are n elements  play video starting at  6 15 and follow transcript6 15  each corresponding to the weight on the particular term   play video starting at  6 21 and follow transcript6 21  so the relevance in this case will be assumed to be the similarity between the two vectors   play video starting at  6 29 and follow transcript6 29  therefore  our ranking function is also defined as the similarity between the query vector and document vector   play video starting at  6 37 and follow transcript6 37  now if i ask you to write a program to implement this approach in a search engine   play video starting at  6 44 and follow transcript6 44  you would realize that this was far from clear  we haven t said a lot of things in detail  therefore it s impossible to actually write the program to implement this  that s why i said  this is a framework   play video starting at  6 59 and follow transcript6 59  and this has to be refined in order to actually  play video starting at  7 4 and follow transcript7 04  suggest a particular ranking function that you can implement on a computer   play video starting at  7 10 and follow transcript7 10  so what does this framework not say  well  it actually hasn t said many things that would be required in order to implement this function   play video starting at  7 24 and follow transcript7 24  first  it did not say how we should define or select the basic concepts exactly   play video starting at  7 32 and follow transcript7 32  we clearly assume the concepts are orthogonal  otherwise  there will be redundancy  for example  if two synonyms or somehow distinguish it as two different concepts  then they would be defining two different dimensions and that would clearly cause redundancy here  or all the emphasizing of matching this concept  because it would be as if you match the two dimensions when you actually matched one semantic concept   play video starting at  8 11 and follow transcript8 11  secondly  it did not say how we exactly should place documents and the query in this space  basically that show you some examples of query and document vectors  but where exactly should the vector for a particular document point to   play video starting at  8 29 and follow transcript8 29  so this is equivalent to how to define the term weights  how do you compute the lose element values in those vectors  this is a very important question  because term weight in the query vector indicates the importance of term   play video starting at  8 48 and follow transcript8 48  so depending on how you assign the weight  you might prefer some terms to be matched over others   play video starting at  8 56 and follow transcript8 56  similarly  the total word in the document is also very meaningful  it indicates how well the term characterizes the document  if you got it wrong then you clearly don t represent this document accurately   play video starting at  9 10 and follow transcript9 10  finally  how to define the similarity measure is also not given  so these questions must be addressed before we can have a operational function that we can actually implement using a program language   play video starting at  9 25 and follow transcript9 25  so how do we solve these problems is the main topic of the next lecture   music 
