pivoted normalized document length pivoted normalized document length  in section 6 3 1 we normalized each document vector by the euclidean length of the vector  so that all document vectors turned into unit vectors  in doing so  we eliminated all information on the length of the original document  this masks some subtleties about longer documents  first  longer documents will   as a result of containing more terms   have higher tf values  second  longer documents contain more distinct terms  these factors can conspire to raise the scores of longer documents  which  at least for some information needs  is unnatural  longer documents can broadly be lumped into two categories   1  verbose documents that essentially repeat the same content   in these  the length of the document does not alter the relative weights of different terms   2  documents covering multiple different topics  in which the search terms probably match small segments of the document but not all of it   in this case  the relative weights of terms are quite different from a single short document that matches the query terms  compensating for this phenomenon is a form of document length normalization that is independent of term and document frequencies  to this end  we introduce a form of normalizing the vector representations of documents in the collection  so that the resulting   normalized   documents are not necessarily of unit length  then  when we compute the dot product score between a  unit  query vector and such a normalized document  the score is skewed to account for the effect of document length on relevance  this form of compensation for document length is known as pivoted document length normalization  
explain nlp   importance of data mining and techniques of data mining with real life examples in nlp data mining helps businesses to extract unknown insights from data  these insights support the decision making processes of businesses  helping them to make data based decisions  this is key for the growth of any organization      when most people hear the term  data mining   they think of numerical data  the reason is that in most cases  data mining involves extracting insights from numerical data  however  this doesn t mean data mining is not applicable to text and voice data  the fact is that text and voice data are rich in information that can be useful for decision making  for example  social media comments about your brand can help you know the thoughts of your customers and prospects towards your brand      nlp  natural language processing  helps data engineers to extracts insights from natural languages such as english  in this article  you will learn how to use nlp in data mining 
tf idf in nlp understanding tf idf in nlp  this post is a collection of structured questions as to why tf idf as a method of text vectorization works  beginning with the primal question on why need it in the first place     why do we need text vectorization    first  computers only understand numbers  any input to the computer in the lowest level  bytecode  is a series of binary numbers  which means that to be able to interpret text from vocal languages  we need to have a mathematical representation for these in numbers   second  a vector representation of text allows us to leverage the power of linear algebra for different operations  dot cross products  planes  perpendiculars  normals etc   this helps in exploratory data analysis  eda   geometric intuition  visualization of the problems  1 d  2 d and 3 d  and extrapolating the linear algebra methods to higher dimensions   3 d    what is the rule for conversion of text to vectors    if three words in english  w1  w2  w3   had vector representations  v1  v2  v3  respectively  then  the rule stated in simple terms is      if  w1  w2  are more semantically related words in english than  w1  w3   then the distance between  v1  v2  vectors should be lesser than the distance between  v1  v3      for example  the words  good  great  are closely related by meaning than  good  bad   so the euclidean distance between  v good   v great   will be smaller than the euclidean distance between  v good   v bad       why do similar words have to be geometrically closer    while training the machine learning models  the model tries to learn and define equations  or conditions  to classify different groups  if similar points are close geometrically  then the it is easier to classify between different groups  the following image helps the visualization of a decision boundary between different labels of data with examples of positive  negative and gender based gender neutral words         grouping of similar words leads to easier decision boundaries  tf idf  tf idf is among the infamous methods  among others  for text vectorization for words in a document     document  the group of words or texts or sentences that represent a single data point  corpus  a collection of documents    tf stands for term frequency  which represents the probability of occurrence of the word in a document       similar to the above definition  we can define the document frequency  which      represents the probability of finding the word across documents in the corpus     idf stands for inverse document frequency  this represents the inverse of document frequency  except for a change   logarithmic transform of the inverse of document frequency       idf gives us a measure of the importance of the word across the corpus  or the dataset   in other words  the words which occur rarely across different documents of the corpus are given higher importance     note that the equation uses a logarithmic transform on the inverse of document frequency  this gives rise to the next question     why do we use logarithmic scale for calculating idf    we can try to reason this in two ways  theoretical and practical     a theoretical explanation  the frequency of occurrence of words in a language  such as english  follows the zipf s law  stated as  given some corpus of natural language utterances  the frequency of any word is inversely proportional to its rank in the frequency table        source  phys org   early frequency distributions for the words in english  with this property of the english language  the probability distribution of frequency of words follows the power law distribution  now  many machine learning algorithms perform better with gaussian distribution of variables  to transform the data from power law distribution to a gaussian distribution  we can use a box cox transform with the logarithmic nature  this possibly explains why a logarithmic transform was proposed for the idf in the research paper     secondly  a more practical intuition is to reduce the range of idf  making it relatively comparable to tf  so that when these two numbers are multiplied in tf idf  the result value is produced with comparable contributions from both     why does the range of tf and idf have to be relatively comparable    consider the words  the  and  hardworking  in english  we know that the latter has more meaning and must be deemed important  the idf  without log transform  of word  the  in a corpus could be 1  how      well  consider a corpus with 1000 documents  if the word  the  occurs in all 1000 documents  then by definition of idf  without log transform  is 1000 1000   1     now  because  hardworking  is a rarely occurring word  the idf  without log transform  of this could be 1000  how      consider the same corpus with 1000 documents  if the word  hardworking  occurred in only one of the documents  then by definition of idf  without log transform  is 1000 1   1000     the range of values of idf  without log transform  is now  1 1000    due to rarely occurring words  however  with a logarithmic transform of base 10  for simplicity   this range is reduced to  1 3      if we multiply tf and idf  without the log transform   then the values are now exploded quickly  due to the huge range of idf   there is a need to dampen the effect of idf in this product     to compensate for the huge range and dampen the effect  we use logarithmic transform on the idf  so that the contribution of tf and idf in the numerical value of tf idf are comparable and within relative range     what happens next after calculating the tf idf value    well  for each word we have a tf idf value  in the vector representation for the document  the value of the dimension relating to each word holds the numerical tf idf value  this vector is used for training  testing and evaluating a model     what are the disadvantages of tf idf    first  we can clearly see that there can be two different words  with the same values of tf idf  because  this metric only takes into account the number of occurrences of the word in the document and corpus     second  it works under the assumption that words with similar meaning  occur similar number of times in the corpus  or  in other words  we can also say that the semantic meaning of word is accounted for by this method  for example  tasty and delicious are two words with the same meaning  however  we cannot guarantee that the tf idf values of these words are relative and similar     well  these cons are overcome by using the algorithm word2vec  proposed in 2013   which proves to be a state of the art technique for text vectorization  saving this technique for the next post     hope this helps       mlearning ai submission suggestions  how to become a writer on mlearning ai  medium com    102      
