lef 2012 evaluation labs and workshop  online working notes  rome  italy  17 20 september 2012  vol  1178   ceurws org   30  p  clough and m  stevenson   developing a corpus of plagiarized short answers   language resources and evaluation  vol  45  no 1  pp  5 24  2011   31  m  l  paramita  p  d  clough  a  aker  a   and r  j  gaizauskas   correlation between similarity measures for inter language linked wikipedia articles   in proceedings of the eighth international conference on language resources and evaluation   lrec 2012   istanbul  turkey  23 25 may 2012  pp  790 797   32  h  asghari  k  khoshnava  o  fatemi  and h  faili   developing bilingual plagiarism detection corpus using sentence aligned parallel corpus   notebook for pan at clef  2015 
text organization ieee websites place cookies on your device to give you the best user experience  by using our websites  you agree to the placement of these cookies  to learn more  read our privacy policy   accept   close  skip to main content    ieee org  ieee xplore  ieee sa  ieee spectrum  more sites  cart   create account  personal sign in    browse   my settings   help     access provided by   university of illinois  sign out          advanced search  conferences  2022 17th iberian conference      machine learning algorithm for text categorization of news articles from senegalese online news websites  publisher  ieee  cite this  pdf  tony tona landu  mamadou bousso  mor absa loum  ousmane sall  lamine faty  yoro dia  ibrahim sawadogo  all authors    25  full  text views    abstract  document sections  i   introduction  ii   related work  iii   methodology  iv   experiments  v   conclusion and future work  authors  figures  references  keywords  metrics  abstract   the growth of information in the form of news articles is a big problem in every society  this information lies in unstructured form and manually managing and effectively making use of it is tedious  in this study  we aimed to build a classifier to categorize news articles from senegalese online news websites according to their contents   politique    religion    justice    sant     education    faits divers   and  people  we developed  based on web scrapping techniques  a crawler for collecting news articles from the senegalese online press  we used ova method to transform our multi labels problem into binary classification problem s  and classify online news articles using different supervised machine learning algorithms we use the area under the roc curve  auc  to measure performances of selected models  most of the themes being recognized correctly with more than 80  of micro average auc  overall  the random forest algorithm showed the best performance with 90  of micro average auc using the collection of data through various senegalese online news portals  a tool to automatically categorize news published articles can be developed with our final prediction model and to guide future research in this area   published in  2022 17th iberian conference on information systems and technologies  cisti   date of conference  22 25 june 2022  date added to ieee xplore  14 july 2022  isbn information   issn information   inspec accession number  21949278  doi  10 23919 cisti54924 2022 9820408  publisher  ieee  conference location  madrid  spain  section i introduction  the introduction of information and communication technologies  icts  and access to the internet in the last decade of the twentieth century has triggered a profound wave of changes in our lives  journalism  as well as the process of mass communication in general  has been completely transformed with the automation of the work process and also the unprecedented increase of information available online through several publishing channels  which become the main source for the creation and large scale dissemination of news articles  consequently  new form of journalism emerged  called multimedia journalism and data journalism  which require journalists to have specific ict skills     senegal  with its large number of internet users  can benefit from multimedia journalism  however  this type of journalism has its challenges  one of the main challenges is to improve the ability for the user to navigate fastly through news articles  in this scenario  automatic categorization of news articles based on thematic can lead to better structuring of textual news article data from various online press websites  and thus provide users with a better online experience  the major challenge in applying automatic classification techniques to news articles is to cope with the velocity  volume  value  variety and veracity  the 5 v s of big data  of these databases  they are constantly updated with news feeds at a high daily frequency     in senegalese online press  news articles are written in french  they are manually categorized by journalists and this leads to the ineffectiveness  search functionality based on thematic  in addition  we denote a lack of support for any type of reporting or dashboards news along thematic axes     to perform automatic text categorization  classical machine learning algorithms can be helpful  the idea is to split the database into to two parts  1  one training data set  to build the classifier  2  one test data set  to test the performance of the built model  however text categorization can be switched from classical machine learning to deep learning by using the recent deep neural network architecture is the transformer  1   in this work  we will use the classical machine learning which still shows high performance in the literature     machine learning  ml   these techniques are doing more and more  smart  things  categorizing text by content thematics  face recognition  speech recognition  named entity recognition  ner   2   fake news detection  3   to name a few  with the rapid growth of online news  news categorization remains an indispensable need to provide users with a good browsing experience when they are online for information extraction  ie  and information retrieval  ir   4   we intend here to present a comprehensive view of ml techniques  in particular supervised learning  sl   by giving a brief overview of the theory underlying this approach  our study is limited to the situations where we wish to model the effect of a set of explanatory variables  x  on a target variable  y   we apply these methods to journalistic data collected through various news sources in the senegalese online press     the main purpose of supervised learning  sl  technique is to learn how to predict a random variable y   y based in a set of explicative random variables denoted by x   x  where y and x depend on the problem at hand but may be through to be respectively   and  d for example  we will often call x the input and y the output  5   the main problem is to find a predictor   f x yx f x   view sourcechosen among the set of all functions      f  x   y      text categorization  the procedure of identifying and classifying documents expressed in a fixed number of predefined categories  such as thematic  is called text categorization or text classification  the main objective of text categorization is to classify a set of documents into a fixed number of predefined categories  each document can belong to one category  we restrict our work to the case of using a supervised learning algorithm to learn classifiers from known examples  labeled documents  and perform categorization automatically on unseen examples  unlabeled documents   mathematically speaking  this situation can be described as follows     consider a set of labeled documents from a source d    d1  d2    dd     d belong to a set of classes c    c1  c2    cn   n      for a given document di text categorization assigns one category cj     text representation  song  f et al   6  proved that the term is the best unit for text representation and classification  since a document is a sequence of words  it is usually represented by an array of words  here we consider the vocabulary or feature set as a set of all the words that appear in the document and can be represented as a binary vector  assigning the value 1 if the document contains the feature word or 0 otherwise  7   therefore  to do text categorization we need to have an effective document representation model such as   bag of word  bow   8   tfidf indexing  9   this is used to form a vector space document representation in order to build an efficient classifier  n gram statistical model is one of the well known representation among the vector space representation  10    11   however  to visualize the representation vector of word in a document in the high dimensional feature vectors of them make powerful information retrieval technologies such as  principal component analysis  pca   12  and t sne  13   unfeasible for large dataset     section ii related work  the existing works on text categorization seems to be challenging when dealing with languages other than english  below  we review the works on text categorization  moreover  we begin with a brief discussion of text categorization in general and specifically some existing works on news articles categorization     a  text categorization  several papers have appeared on text categorization because of the increasing availability of the important numbers of channels which generate much data in the form of online news articles  social media  and so on  online news channels grow fast around the world and lead to an active research community doing some comprehensive work on text categorization due to some major challenges like fake news detection  questions answering system  14   named entity linking  15   and so on     some of former work in text categorization focused on the use of machine learning techniques  m  lkonomakis et al  illustrated the text categorization process using machine learning techniques  the reference cited the major theoretical issues and guide the researcher to interesting research direction  their conclusion indicated that the text classification problem is an artificial intelligence research topic  especially given the vast number of documents available in the form of web pages and other electronic texts like emails  discussion forum postings and other electronic documents  this conclusion supports our decision to have our work carry the text categorization from the senegalese news websites     armand joulin et al  claimed that fasttext  16  is often on par with deep learning classifiers in terms of accuracy  and many orders of magnitude faster for training and evaluation  17   they tested scalability of their approach  further evaluation is carried on the yfcc100 m dataset  18  and obtained the overall of their accuracy goes up to 97 1   andrew mccallum et al  aimed to compare the multivariate bernoulli model and the multinomial model by describing the differences and details of these two models  and by empirically comparing their classification performance on five text corpora  they find that multivariate bernoulli performs well with small vocabulary sizes  but that the multinomial usually performs even better at larger vocabulary sizes providing on average a 27  reduction in error over the multivariate bernoulli model at any vocabulary size  19      b  similar works  sarr et al   20  is perhaps the closest work to ours  since they also did the text categorization of news articles by thematic from senegalese online news websites but they did not use machine learning algorithms  in their work  they proposed textblob for text categorization from senegalese news websites  their experiments show that textblob  21  is a good classifier with 94  in term of precision  abu et al   22  and inoshika et al   23  only trained the bayes classifier and svm classifier  moreover  they do not discuss other machine learning techniques  which also perform the task of news article categorization and can potentially perform better in predicting the categories of new observations     section iii methodology  the methodology follows in this research aimed to design an approach for senegalese news text categorization from multiple senegalese online news sources  the methodology consists of four major modules  selected the senegalese news article collection step  data preprocessing step  data representation step  and senegalese news article classify step  fig  1 shows the outline of this senegalese news classifier       figure 1   outline of the senegalese news categorization methodology    show all    a  data collection  to carry out our supervised learning task  it was necessary to collect a large number of news articles related to their thematic content through different senegalese online press portals using web crawler  then a crawler  that we developed  was executed for each individual news websites source  we use it to collect all the components of online press articles like titles  summaries  contents  links  and so on  the crawler collected online press articles from various online press websites every day  by hour intervals per day  the data collection was supervised by one of the team members to ensure data format  the page format of each news source was analyzed  and common patterns were identified  since there is no constant pattern for the design of news web pages  and it varies from one provider to another     b  dataset and data pre processing  we used the corpus of news data from senegalese news websites  contains text from 68 050 news articles in different thematics  29 843  politique   15 588  sant    7 539  people   5885  faits divers   5 597  justice   and 3 598  r ligion   we considered here six articles thematics labelled as    justice     people     politique     faits divers     sant    and   r ligion   once the content of the url is downloaded and saved to elastic search database  it becomes challenging to extraction information of the news     before creating a feature from the raw data  we need to do some data preprocessing to ensure the quality of our data set  this is because the data are from several collection sources and probably contains unnecessary and noisy information  however  we follow the standard data preprocessing steps most commonly found in the literature  24   which includes     special character cleaning from senegalese news content  special characters such as double quotes  etc  have to be removed from the document since we aren t expecting any predicting power from them may actually damage the classification performance     upcase downcase senegalese news content  we need to upcase downcase the document because we expect to reduce the size of the considerable vector elements  for example   information  and  information  to be the same word  for that reason  we have down cased every word     punctuation signs  characters such as                     and others punctuation signs have to be removed because these non significant characters represent noise     possessive pronouns  we would expect that  le mien    le sien  and  le tien  had the same predicting power     stemming or lemmatization  stemming is the way of splitting derived word to its constituent root  however  lemmatization is the way of reducing the word to its lemma  the difference between both methods is that lemmatization provides existing words  whereas stemming provides the root  which may not be an existing word  for this task we have used both and a lemmatizer based in wordnet     stop words  words such as  le  or  la    de   and so on won t have any predicting power since they will presumably be common to all the documents  for this reason  they may represent noise that can be eliminated  for the purpose of this research  we have downloaded a list of french stop words from the natural language toolkit nltk  and spacy python package and establish the general stop word list for senegalese news then deleted them from the corpus since they carry almost no significant information to the corpus because they are used just because of the grammar     the dataset contains many redundancies  additional step required to filter out redundant articles     c  explanatory data analysis and visualization  we conduct out an exploratory analysis of the dataset in order to gain some significant insightful could impact the text classification task  however  one of our main concerns when developing a classification model is whether the different classes are balanced  this means that the dataset contains an approximately equal portion of each label     looking at our data in fig 2 we can get the distribution of articles in each the different values of label category      figure 2   the distribution of articles in each the different percentages of label category     show all    fig 2 shows the distribution of the collected dataset among the different categories  we can observe that 43 85  of the articles in our dataset are in the  politique  thematic and the other side 22 91  in the  sant   thematic area  collectively  these two thematic areas cover more than half of our entire dataset  on the other hand  articles on people   religion    faitsdivers  and  justice  have approximately the same distribution and are rare  therefore  we can clearly see that our dataset is unbalanced  this can create an example of an accuracy paradox in which our accuracy is high but we will predict all the same classes incorrectly  therefore  we considered the confusion matrices and roc and area under the roc curve  auc  in order to see the performance of our model  since the accuracy metric is not a good metric for unbalanced data sets  then for more detail on these metrics see section iii e     another interesting variable can be the text length of the news articles  we can obtain the distribution of the text length by categories in fig 3     we can see in fig 3 that  people  article tend to be longer than others  we can also see in fig 4  fig 5  fig 6  fig 7  fig 8  and fig 9 the word cloud of categories in order to see the most word dominant to each category       figure 3   the length distribution across each category of article    show all      figure 4   word cloud for faits divers    show all      figure 5   word cloud for people    show all      figure 6   word cloud for justice    show all      figure 7   word cloud for  politique     show all      figure 8   word cloud for  r ligion      show all      figure 9   word cloud for  sant      show all    d  feature engineering  feature engineering is an essential part before building the algorithms  it is also the process of using the domain knowledge of the data to create features that will make the learning algorithm works  so  for the underlying problem  the inputs need to be transformed into good quality features and help improve the performance of the model     several common approaches are proposed in the literature when dealing with textual data to obtain features that represent the data  we will discuss  in this paper  the common methods that are most suitable for our task     text representation  there are a variety of methods that can be used to analyze news articles from senegalese news websites  one of the most popular is to use a vector representation of the article where each term is indexed and then weighted  in this paper  we use a method called tf idf to represent our dataset where each row in the dataset is a single document in the corpus  this technique assigns importance to the determinants and prepositions that contribute little to the overall meaning of the item  in this approach  frequent and infrequent words are often filtered out of the original text  and sometimes words appearing in a list of empty words are removed  e g    le    la    la    tf idf is a combination of two words  term frequency  tf  is used to measure the number of times a term occurs in a document  and inverse document frequency  idf assigns a lower weight to frequent words and a higher weight to infrequent words   25   so the tf idf can therefore be calculated as follows   tfidf t d d  tf t d  idf t d   1   view sourcewhere  tf t d  log 1 freq t d  idf t d  log ncount d d t d     view sourceand word t      document d    d  with n is the number of documents in the corpus  tf is the term frequency of word t in document d  freq t  d  is the raw frequency of the most frequent word in a document and idf is the inverse document frequency of word t in the corpus d  count d   d  t   d  is the number of documents that contain a word     feature transformation  feature transformation is one of the different steps of feature engineering which aim to reduce the feature size  26   this approach doesn t weight terms in order to discard the lower weighted but ensure that it conveys similar information concisely based on the feature concurrencies     there are many applications of dimension reduction techniques in machine learning  by reducing the dimensional space to 2 or 3 dimensions map that contain a great part of the information  we can plot our data points and be able to recognize some patterns as humans using visualization     in this paper we have used two well known methods for feature transformation  principal component analysis  pca   and the t distributed stochastic neighbor embedding  t sne   principal component analysis aims to learn a discriminative transformation matrix which reduce the initial feature space into lower dimensional feature space in order to reduce the complexity of the classification task without any trade off in accuracy  27   whereas  t sne is a probabilistic technique particularly well suited for visualization of high dimensional data sets  it minimizes pairwise similarities of the input objects and a distribution that measures pairwise similarities of the corresponding low dimensional points in the embedding     the plots are shown in fig 10 and fig 11  we can see that using t sne technique makes it easier to distinguish the different classes  although in this paper  we have only used dimensionality reduction techniques for the purposes of visualization       figure 10   pca to distinguish the different classes    show all      figure 11   t sne to distinguish the different classes    show all    e  implementation  we describe here the details of the implementation setup used in this research     to build our classifier  we used the python language  the most common programming language for performing machine learning tasks  in particular text categorization  and implemented scikit learn  the most used python library for such tasks  28   since some algorithms are designed for binary classification problems such as support vector machine method  svm   logistic regression method  lrm   we use one vs rest for multi class classification method which is a heuristic method for using binary classification algorithms for multi class classification  29   therefore  we create a dictionary to assign each label a numerical id as describe in table i below     table i  table of numerical id    since we are following a supervising learning approach  we need to set apart a test set in order to prove the quality of our models when predicting unseen data  we have chosen a random split with 75  of the observations as training data set and 25  of the observations as test data set  we will perform the hyperparameter tuning process with cross validation in the training data  fit the final model to it  30      f  evaluation metrics  as mentioned in iii e  after performing the hyperparameter tuning process with the training data with cross validation and fitting the model to this training data  we need to evaluate its performance on the test set so as to obtain an evaluation metric as less biased as possible     to evaluate the performance of the classifiers  we use several common metrics proposed in the literature to gain insight how good the model is performing when dealing with multi class  31    32   we are used the accuracy when comparing the models and when choosing the best hyperparameters  the confusion matrix and the classification report  which computes precision  recall and f1 score to induce models for predicting the categories of articles  random forest method  k nearest neighbors  knn  method  adaboost method  sgd classifier  decision tree method  gaussian naive bayes method  dummy method  in each case we used the default settings  we used 5 fold cross validation for all experiments and all results are based on these ten runs     section iv experiments  the following experiments compare the performance of random forest and sgd classifier with other conventional learning methods commonly used for the text categorization  each method represents machine learning algorithm  k nearest neighbor classifier  adaboost  decision tree  gaussian naive bayes  dummy classifier carried out with the default parameter  random forest and sgd classifier are cared out with the best parameters after turning parameters process  the results of our experiments can be seen in table ii with the best achieved values highlighted  this table specifies the accuracy  precision  recall  and f1 socre for each of the seven learning algorithms  the baseline strategy allows us to consider the degree of class imbalance when evaluating the performance of the text categorization     table ii  table of performance for each algorithm in predicting news articles labels    table ii shows the comparison of parameters like precision accuracy score  precision  recall and f1 score  we choose the models for future analysis based on performance described in table ii  our results indicate that none of the seven learning algorithms consistently performs well  but the random forest classifier has overall the best performance  more results presented in fig 12  fig 13  fig 14  and fig 15  which show the confusion matrices and roc curve associated with each of the best learning algorithms  for each of the labels thematics       figure 12   confusion matrix for random forest    show all      figure 13   confusion matrix for sgd classifier    show all      figure 14   roc curve auc for random forest    show all      figure 15   roc curve auc for sgd classifier    show all    fig  14 and fig  15 show that in most cases  the evaluation metrics  confirming its appropriateness for our hand s problems  however  the two most common thematics   r ligion  and  sant    we generally achieve accuracies above 90    sant   appears easier to identify than  religion   which seems to make sense  since  sant   involves more extreme changes in this last period because of covid 19  it appears much more difficult to identify the two thematics  politique  and  justice   but as we shall see shortly  that is because those two thematics are often confused with another  note that although there are very few examples of faits divers and people  we can still identify these thematics quite well  because as noted earlier  the two thematics cause the classifier to not easily detected from their content     section v conclusion and future work  in this paper  we have described how machine learning can be used to perform categorization of news articles published in the senegalese press  we have also shown that the categorization of news articles in the senegalese press can be very accurate  eighty percent of the articles are correctly labelled with the random forest model     our work would not have been possible without the collection of data through various senegalese online news portals  we plan to improve our task of categorizing news articles published in the senegalese online press by thematic in several ways  1  learning to recognize additional thematic news items  such as  sports     conomie   etc   2  obtaining more training data from a larger number of senegalese news sources  in addition  we plan to categorize news articles using unsupervised algorithms and compare their results with ones of the supervised models  one of the main advantages of this approach is that the process of partitioning a data set into natural groups without any human annotation  this eliminates the problem of mislabeling news articles published in the senegalese online press from human annotations     acknowledgment  the authors would like to thank the anonymous reviewers for the insightful comments on the preliminary version  this work is totaly supported by the tona s family under the grants asovena ngo     authors  figures  references  keywords  metrics  more like this  an intelligent web portal system for web information region integration  2005 ieee international conference on systems  man and cybernetics    published  2005    information