wikipedia page on mutual information in probability theory and information theory  the mutual information  mi  of two random variables is a measure of the mutual dependence between the two variables  more specifically  it quantifies the  amount of information   in units such as shannons  bits   nats or hartleys  obtained about one random variable by observing the other random variable  the concept of mutual information is intimately linked to that of entropy of a random variable  a fundamental notion in information theory that quantifies the expected  amount of information  held in a random variable     not limited to real valued random variables and linear dependence like the correlation coefficient  mi is more general and determines how different the joint distribution of the pair   displaystyle  x y   is from the product of the marginal distributions of   displaystyle x  and   displaystyle y   mi is the expected value of the pointwise mutual information  pmi      the quantity was defined and analyzed by claude shannon in his landmark paper  a mathematical theory of communication   although he did not call it  mutual information   this term was coined later by robert fano  2  mutual information is also known as information gain 
text feature extraction we are using cookies to give you the best experience on our website  but you are free to manage these at any time  to continue with our standard settings click  accept   to find out more and manage your cookies  click  manage cookies      accept   manage cookies    skip to main content  search    submit  advanced search  home  journals  aslib journal of information management  volume 74 issue 4  albert bpf  a book purchase forecast model for university library by using albert for text feature extraction  albert bpf  a book purchase forecast model for university library by using albert for text feature extraction  yejun wu  xiaxian wang  peilin yu  yongkai huang   aslib journal of information management    issn  2050 3806    article publication date  31 january 2022     issue publication date  10 june 2022    downloads  73     pdf  408 kb   abstract  introduction  literature review  methods  experiments  experiments and results  discussion  conclusion  abstract  purpose  the purpose of this research is to achieve automatic and accurate book purchase forecasts for the university libraries and improve efficiency of manual book purchase     design methodology approach  the authors presented a book purchase forecast model with a lite bert albert bpf  to achieve their goals  first  the authors process all the book data to unify format of books  features  such as isbn  title  authors  brief introduction and so on  second  they exploit the book order data to label all books supplied by booksellers with  purchased  or  non purchased   the labelled data will be used for model training  last  the authors regard the book purchase task as a text classification problem and present a model named albert bpf  which applies albert to extract text features of books and bpf classification layer to forecast purchased books  to solve the problem     findings  the application of deep learning in book purchase task is effective  the data the authors exploited are the historical book purchase data from their university library  the authors  experiments on the data show that albert bpf can seek out the books that need to be purchased with an accuracy of over 82   and the highest accuracy reached is 88 06   these indicate that the deep learning model is sufficient to assist the traditional manual book purchase way     originality value  this research applies albert  which is based on the latest natural language processing  nlp  architecture transformer  to library book purchase task     keywords  digital libraries book purchase nlp text classification neural networks deep learning  citation  wu  y   wang  x   yu  p  and huang  y   2022    albert bpf  a book purchase forecast model for university library by using albert for text feature extraction   aslib journal of information management  vol  74 no  4  pp  673 687  https   doi org 10 1108 ajim 04 2021 0114     download as  ris  publisher  emerald publishing limited  copyright   2021  emerald publishing limited    introduction  as the world has entered the information age  varied information which denotes human intellectual achievement is constantly converted into electronic resources  including books  li and jiang  2019   but for libraries  paper books are still an essential component  therefore  book purchase is an important task that cannot be avoided for libraries  university libraries are not exception  as students  we have conducted research on the task of book purchase in our school library   at present  most libraries have relatively few big data applications  and they are still in the era of manual information processing  anna and mannan  2020   unlike buying books for individuals  book purchase is a heavy workload for university libraries  purchasers are required to select suitable books from large amounts of books which are on sale for bulk purchase  in china  most university libraries still adopt manual method to purchase books  there are usually two ways to do this  the first one is the book pre purchase scheme  first of all  booksellers provide basic information about all books for sale  including titles  authors  brief introduction and so on  then the purchasers analyse the basic information of all books and combine the actual demands of the university libraries and the previous book purchase experience to select the appropriate book  finally  purchasers confirm the list of books which university libraries need to purchase and submit to booksellers for purchasing  another method is purchasing books on site  purchasers go directly to bookstores to check the physical books to determine whether they need to buy them  purchasers usually go to a number of physical bookstores to screen and then determine a book purchase list  finally  purchasers send the purchase list to booksellers and complete book purchase  it is not difficult to see from the above methods that the book purchase task requires purchasers to spend a lot of time and energy on books screening   in addition  there are some uncertainties in traditional purely manual book purchase methods  purchasers usually make book screening based on their own understanding and occasional readers  feedback to them  and the data of books  basic information provided by booksellers are often enormous  and it is difficult for purchasers to effectively and accurately analyse such a large amount of book information  this means that purchasers  work status at that time and personal preferences of them will greatly affect the book purchase  sometimes some of the books they identified may not meet the demands of the library  obviously  this low efficiency purely manual book purchase method is no longer suitable for the current environment  therefore  how to make book purchase more automatic and intelligent has become an urgent issue to be solved  wang and lei  2018    therefore  we began to study how to quickly and accurately screen books in order to help purchasers complete the book purchase task more efficiently  we found there are currently some methods that have reached a certain level of automation  purchasers can automatically generate book orders through relatively simple operations  but these methods have some disadvantages  such as using the keyword mapping algorithm  chenglin  2013   this method does not make good use of the purchasers  previous purchase experience  in addition  a single hidden layer neural network has  liqin and feng  2015  been used to predict the number of purchased books  but this type of network uses only part of the book information  and much information remains unused  in our previous research  we used a book purchase forecast  bpf  model based on the convolutional neural network  cnn   guo et al   2020   which mainly used the title  authors and keywords information to complete the bpf   after that  we have been thinking about changing the model to improve the performance of the model  we noticed that the book information contains a lot of text features  and the technology in the field of nlp can handle these text features well  moreover  the brief introduction is a summary of the full text information of the book  which can well reflect the features of the book  so in this research  we focus on the features extraction of brief introduction  due to the popularity of transformer based models in the nlp field in recent years  we soon thought of using transformer based model for text features extraction  also  the model with transformer architecture is particularly good at long text analysis  however  models based on the transformer architecture often have huge amounts of parameters and require huge amount of computing resources  after comprehensive consideration  we selected the model with relatively small scale  albert  for text feature extraction  then we use the extracted text features for subsequent bpf tasks  the main contributions of our work are as follows     we applied the model albert which is based on the transformer architecture to extract the text information of the book purchase task  and we presented a bpf module to predict the books that are need to purchase by utilizing extracted text features     we processed the real book data provided by our university library and trained the model on the real data  in the end  the highest accuracy of our model reached 88 06      we compared albert bpf model with the traditional model and our previous model on the same data  and we found that albert bpf model has the best and most stable performance     literature review  intelligent book purchase and book recommendation  as early as years ago  there were some research studies on the book purchase task  in 2013  chenglin  2013  presented an automatic generation model of book purchase list based on keyword mapping  they realized that the method of determining the book purchase list through traditional manual statistics is inefficient  and they started an attempt to automatically generate the book purchase list  their attempts have achieved certain results  in this research  they use the experience of experts in the field to determine keywords and then filter and map books based on the keywords  their idea is similar to an expert system  which is an application of early artificial intelligence  but in the book purchase task  the title and introduction can better reflect the characteristics of the book  the author is also an important factor when deciding whether a book needs to be purchased  however  due to the limitations of text processing technology at the time  they only used the keyword information of the books   in 2012  krizhevsky et al   2012  presented alexnet  which greatly promoted the development of artificial intelligence  since then  more and more people have paid attention to artificial intelligence and artificial neural networks and used them to solve a series of problems  so do liqin and feng  2015   in 2015  they presented a method of using a single hidden layer neural network to solve the book purchase task  they use the four features of books  source  popularity  professional relevance and price  as the input of the neural network to implement book purchase list recommendation  they introduced neural networks to process book purchase task  which was a very advanced idea at the time  but their model hardly uses the text information of the book itself  what is more  they only use a single hidden layer neural network  all of these limit the accuracy of their models   our team noticed the importance of the text information of the book and presented cnn book  guo et al   2020  in 2020 to solve the book purchase task by processing the text information  cnn book applies text cnn  kim  2014  to extract text features of books  based on it  we have achieved a relatively good book purchase accuracy  however  the text extraction capabilities of cnn based model are insufficient  so at that time  we could only give up the extraction of long text features of the introduction and replace it by making full use of the title  author and keywords  recently  with the rapid development of deep learning and nlp  we began to use the transformer architecture  which has recently shined in the field of nlp  to improve the model to cope with long text features   book recommendation is a classic research field  which is related to book purchase prediction  we have also learned about some research in the field of book recommendation  collaborative filtering is one of the most important technologies in the field of recommendation systems  ramakrishnan et al   2020  improved the book recommendation system mainly by introducing implicit user feedback into collaborative filtering  mathew et al   2016  proposed a book recommendation system  which combined content based filtering  collaborative filtering and association rule mining  and produced efficient and effective book recommendations  ng  2020  mainly focussed on children s book recommendations  they integrated the matrix factorization approach and the content based approach to predict the grade levels of books and produce book recommendations for children  wang et al   2021  proposed a cluster based reinforcement learning algorithm for book recommendation  which achieved good results  ng and jung  2020  used a deep learning model and various metadata of books to produce book recommendations  vaz et al   2012  proposed a hybrid literary book recommendation system through author ranking   literature about nlp  in the early days  nlp mainly used traditional statistical methods  the most typical one is the n gram model  cavnar and trenkle  1994   however  this model has poor performance on language long distance dependence  and the traditional text structuring method has various shortcomings  in 2003  bengio et al   2003  proposed the neural language model  nnlm  it implements the distributed representation of words and can carry out language long distance dependence modelling in a certain extent  however  nnlm only represents the text on the left side of the word  which means that the obtained representation is not sufficient  mikolov et al   2010  improved the nnlm and presented a language model rnnlm based on rnn  which was used in speech recognition tasks  which greatly improved the recognition accuracy  in 2013  mikolov et al   2013  presented the word2vec model  which made large scale word embedding possible   in 2014  an important structure of nlp was born  sutskever et al   2014  presented a sequence to sequence structure  this is a general framework that uses neural networks to map one sequence to another  the model inputs a sequence  generating a deep representation vector through the encoding layer  and then outputs a target sequence by decoding the deep representation vector in decoding layer  this is the sequence to sequence structure  this structure is naturally suitable for translation tasks  in essence  the encoding layer is an extraction module for text information  and the deep features extracted by encoding layer can be used to solve various tasks of nlp   in 2015  chorowski et al   2015  presented the attention mechanism which became another important component of nowadays nlp  the attention mechanism is used in all aspects of the nlp field rapidly and has achieved good results  the attention has been even introduced into the field of computer vision  the effectiveness of attention mechanism caught vaswani et al   2017  s eyes  they presented a sequence to sequence architecture  transformer  based entirely on attention in 2017  all layers of transformer are composed of self attention modules improved by the attention mechanism  compared with the previous model  transformer has made great progress in various nlp tasks   2018 is a milestone year for nlp  the google team presented a pre trained language model  bert  devlin et al   2018   based on transformer  and successfully achieved the results of sota state of the art in 11 nlp tasks  since then  pre trained language models based on transformer have begun to dominate the nlp field  models based on the transformer architecture even begin to appear in the computer vision  these all mean the powerful feature extraction capabilities of the transformer  carion et al   2020  chen et al   2021  dosovitskiy et al   2020   so  this time we try to use the transformer architecture to extract the text features of the book for book purchase model   methods  in this section  we will introduce our problem definition for the book purchase task firstly and then describe the structure of the presented albert bpf model  which is shown in figure 1  the description will be divided into two parts  book text feature extraction based on albert and bpf   problem definition  there is a key issue in the book pre purchase method to purchase books  how do we choose the books that the library needs  especially in the large amount of book data provided by booksellers  we use the final order book list of the university library to add labels  purchase or not purchase  for all books provided by the bookseller  since the book feature information is mainly composed of text features  we can define such a model  the input is the various text features of the book  and the output is label  purchase or not purchase  in this way  the book purchase task can be regarded as a text classification task  therefore  we set the goal of the book purchase task as  establish a model  input the text features of the book and finally  classify the books into purchased books and non purchased books as accurately as possible     book text feature extraction based on albert  we apply the pre trained model albert  lan et al   2019  as the underlying structure of our model  we apply it to extract book text features and generate deep vector representations of book text features  the feature representation vector will be used to forecast the purchased books in the bpf module   tokenization module  albert applies the same tokenization as bert  passing through tokenization module  the text information of book is transformed into a sequence of lexical items  for chinese  each lexical item is basic chinese character  for english  each lexical item is sub word in the vocabulary  defined by bert   the sequence of lexical items after tokenization is used as the input of embedding module     embedding module  for each lexical item in the sequence of lexical items  it is mapped to a unique corresponding vector eit  token embeddings  which is used as initial vector representation of input  then we set the segment position identifier for each lexical item and get segment embedding vector eis  segment embeddings   also  we embed the absolute position of each lexical item to obtain a position embedding vector eip  position embeddings   the dimensions of these three vectors are the same  we set it as embeddingsize  finally  we add the three embedding vectors and obtain the final embedding vector ei  embeddingsize     ei eit eis eip  transformer module  the main coding layers of albert adopt stacked 12 layer transformer  as shown in figure 1  each layer uses a multi head attention mechanism and a shared feed forward neural network  and the attention layer and feed forward neural network layer both use residual connection and regularization   y norm x layer x     where x is the input  y is the output  layer is a specific network layer and norm   is a normalization layer   multi head attention layer  first  we let the text embedding vector matrix e pass through three linear mapping layers of the same size  this operation is only required in the first transformer layer   and get three matrices of the same dimension  q k v     q e wq k e wk v e wv   where e  e1   ei  en t e  n embeddingsize wq wk wv  embeddingsize hiddensize q k v  n hiddensize  then for q k and v  we respectively apply h linear mappings of the same size  h represents the number of heads of the multi head attention mechanism  to obtain h groups of matrices  qi ki vi  i  1     h     qi q wiq ki k wik vi v wiv   where wiq wik wiv  hiddensize hiddensizeh qi ki vi  n hiddensizeh  then we calculate the attention weighting matrix ai for the h groups of qi ki vi  respectively   ai attention qi ki vi  softmax qi kitdk  vi   where ai  n hiddensizeh  dk hiddensizeh  dk denotes the dimensions of qi and ki  the operation of dividing by dk is to avoid the calculated similarity value getting too large  finally  the h groups of attention weighting matrices are concatenated and passed through a linear mapping layer to obtain the complete attention matrix a   a multihead q k v  concat a1   ai   ah  w   where w  hiddensize hiddensize a  n hiddensize   feed forward neural network layer  this layer uses a relu layer to connect two layers of linear transformation     h fnn a  max 0 a w1 b1  w2 b2   where w1 w2  hiddensize hiddensize h  n hiddensize  we denote the output of the i th transformer layer as hi   when we input the word embedding vector into the transformer module  in the first transformer layer we set     q k v e w   and in the second to 12th transformer layer we set   qi ki vi hi 1   that is  q k v are shared in each layer of the transformer  in addition  these 12 layers of transformer also share parameters  which means the corresponding parameters of each layer of transformer remain the same  to lower calculation expenses  finally  we get the text s deep vector representation matrix h12  n hiddensize extracted by the albert layer  the matrix h12 is composed of n representation vectors  and every vector in h12 contains the context of all words  so we only need to use one representation vector of h12 as the deep vector representation of the full input text  we select the first vector of n representation vectors   h12 1  then we use  h12 1 as the input vector of bpf module   bpf  book purchase forecast  after the albert text feature extraction module  the text feature of the book is represented as a vector ht with a dimension of hiddensize  the bpf module classifies the text of the book to forecast whether the book will be purchased  the structure of bpf is shown in figure 1  first  we use two fully connected network layers  also connected with the relu activation function in the middle  to map the vector ht into two classification scores  then we normalize the classification scores by softmax to obtain the classification probability     s fnn ht  max 0 ht w1bpf b1bpf  w2bpf b2bpf p softmax s    where w1bpf  hiddensize bpfhiddensize w2bpf  bpfhiddensize 2 s p  2  we use the cross entropy loss l as the loss of the full model  the specific formula is as follows   l   ylog p0   1 y log p1     where y is label  p  p0 p1   y 1 means purchasing  and y 0 means not purchasing   experiments  model flow  as shown in figure 2  the flow of our method is mainly divided into two parts  training and predicting  in the training part  we pre process the book data firstly  then the pre processed data are inputted into the initialized model for training  finally  we save the trained model parameters  in the predicting part  we also pre process the test data of book  or new book data   which helps input data into model properly  then we load the parameters which were saved in the training part into the model  finally  we use the model to calculate and obtain the prediction of book purchase order   we evaluate the model by comparing the output of predicting part and the actual labels  our method is based on text classification and is a binary classification problem  purchase or not purchase   therefore  we apply the commonly used evaluation indicators of the binary classification model  accuracy  precision  recall and f1 value  the accuracy is the ratio of the number of samples that are correctly predicted to the total number of samples  it is the most commonly used indicator  which can generally measure the performance of a forecast  precision is the ratio of the number of positive samples correctly classified by the model to the total number of positive samples classified by the model  in our case  if the model has high precision  the model tends to cautiously find the books that need to be purchased  it means that the model would rather omit a book that needs to be purchased than predict a book that does not need to be purchased as a book that needs to be purchased  recall is the ratio of the number of correctly classified positive samples to the number of all positive samples  in our case  the model with high recall tends to find as many books as possible that need to be purchased  f1 is an indicator that comprehensively considers precision and recall     dataset and data pre processing  the data used in our experiments come from the book data of our university library from 2010 to 2016  it is divided into two categories  the book list with detailed information of each book provided by the bookseller  and the final book order data determined by the book purchasers  table 1 gives an example of book with detailed information  all of the book data are stored in excel format  the first row of each excel lists the various attributes of the book  such as isbn number  book title  language  content introduction  author and so on  any of the rest rows is the specific attribute data of each book   the first step of data pre processing is to clean the data  we found that in the book data provided by different booksellers  types and quantities of book attributes are different  therefore  we only retain the text attributes which they all have  title  author  brief introduction and topic words  also  we retain the isbn number of the book to be used for matching in the next step  we performed the above operations on all book data and finally obtained the total book data and the order data for each year from 2010 to 2016  in order to facilitate the input  we save it in csv file format      but the cleaned data cannot be directly trained on the model  we also need to label the total book data  we match the total book data and order data of each year after cleaning  during the cleaning  we retained the isbn number of each book  and the isbn is unique to each book  therefore  we can use the isbn number to merge the total book data and the order data and then mark the matched books as 1  and the unmatched books as 0  finally  we obtain the labelled book data of every year     we analysed the label data of every year and found there is a large gap of proportion between purchased books and non purchased books  shown in table 2   such unbalanced data are not conducive to classification tasks  therefore  before training  we must perform data enhancement operations to keep the ratio of the two classes of book data at 1 1  we use sampling operations to achieve this  first  we assume the data size of every year is n  on this basis  we let positive and negative samples  i e  purchased books and non purchased books  account for 50  each  then we use random sampling in the labelled data to get n 2 size positive and negative samples  respectively  it can be seen from table 2 that the proportion of purchased books is far less than 50   and the proportion of non purchased books is much greater than 50   so  we perform sampling with replacement on the positive samples  and sampling without replacement on the negative samples  finally  we obtained the balanced sample data set of each year  in order to facilitate evaluation and testing of the model  we divide the balanced data set into a training set and a test set at 8 2  one is used for training  and another is used for evaluation of the model   training parameters  dimension configuration  in our model  the largest text input is 250 words  the text more than 250 words will be cut off  and the text less than 250 words will be filled with 0 to ensure the input dimension is constant  for the embedding module  the output dimension of our model is 128  embeddingsize 128  for the transformer module  the hidden layer size is 768  hiddensize 768  we use 12 multi head attention  h 12  for the bpf module  we set the hidden layer size to 80  bpfhiddensize 80     initialization parameters  for the weight matrix of the transformer layer  we initialize it with the pre trained parameters of the albert base model  lan et al   2019   for the weight matrix of the bpf layer  we use the random initialization of the standard normal distribution   optimization strategy  we use adamw algorithm  loshchilov and hutter  2018  to optimize and update the weight parameters  the transformer layers apply a learning rate of 0 00001  and the bpf layers apply a learning rate of 0 001  all weight parameters are subject to l2 regularization penalty to ensure the generalization ability of the model   experiments and results  model evaluation experiment  we use the test data set generated in data pre processing for model evaluation  we test each year s data separately and calculate the accuracy  precision  recall and f1 value  the results are shown in table 3  in the table  we can see that our model can stabilize at an accuracy of over 80   and the highest rate reaches 88 06   our highest recall reached 95 01   indicating that our model can find books that need to be purchased as much as possible  and the precision and f1 value have a good performance  basically stable above 80    model comparison experiment  for comparison  we conducted experiments on the traditional methods  cnn book which is presented in our previous work  guo et al   2020  and albert bpf  the traditional methods used pv dbow pv dm with traditional classification  we applied pv dm and pv dbow  le and mikolov  2014  to generate representation vectors  and then we used the traditional machine learning models  such as decision tree  safavian and landgrebe  1991   logistic regression  hosmer et al   2013   random forest  biau and scornet  2016  and support vector machine  ding et al   2011   to do book purchase classification  for cnn book  we used the same training data set to train it and also tested it on the testing data set  table 4 shows the average performance comparison in different models  from the table  we can see that our models  including cnn book and albert bpf  are far superior to the traditional methods  what is more  albert bpf has the best performance in the test  because it has the best performance on recall and f1 with high precision   detailed comparison with cnn book  in order to analyse the performance of cnn book and albert bpf  we made a detailed comparison of the two models on the test data of all years  table 5 shows the results of albert bpf and cnn book on test data from 2010 to 2016  and figure 3 shows the detailed performance comparison between cnn book and albert bpf  it can be seen from the figure that the accuracy and f1 value of alber bpf are better than those of cnn book in the whole process and are over above 80   for recall  alber bpf is better than cnn book in terms of stability and value  we can see the recall of cnn book is not stable and often has some low values  it means that cnn book cannot find all the books that need to be purchased as much as possible  which is detrimental to library book purchase task  for precision  albert bpf is slightly lower than cnn book  but it is very stable  after analysis  we knew the reason why albert bpf had slightly lower precision  it is that albert tends to choose books  which need to be purchased  as much as possible  i e  high recall rate   we can see that in the 2014 test set  the recall of cnn book reached an awesome 97   but its precision dropped drastically  only about 70   this also confirms our analysis  by comparison  it can be found that the albert bpf model has improved significantly as a whole and achieved a high precision in the case of a high recall   ablation experiment  in order to analyse the importance of the brief introduction information for bpf  we conducted a simple ablation experiment  we retrained the albert bpf model  but used the missing brief introduction data for training and testing  table 6 shows the experiment results of albert bpf without brief introduction  we can see that after removing the brief introduction information of the book  the accuracy of the model drops drastically  almost all performance indicators are less than 70   which means that the model is only better than guessing  almost all performance indicators are below 70   which means that the performance of albert bpf without brief introduction is almost only similar to traditional machine learning models  it can be seen that adding brief information greatly improves the accuracy of the model s prediction of book purchases   discussion  we have conducted a detailed study on the book purchase task of university library  aiming to help book purchasers with automated and efficient book purchase assistance  we have made a preliminary attempt in our previous work and achieved some results  in this study  we focussed on the book brief introduction part that was not included in our previous work  we replaced the text feature processing model so that we can better extract the long text features of the brief introduction part to help us make better book purchase predictions  we have conducted a series of experiments  and the results of the experiments show that the addition of book brief introduction part has greatly improved the accuracy of the model book purchase prediction  the accuracy of the model is over 82   and the highest is 88 06   such a stable and high accuracy rate indicates that the model is sufficient to assist book purchasers in their book purchase task     why is our model able to improve the accuracy so much than our previous work  we think it is mainly related to two factors  the first is the increase of book feature information  obviously  we have added the book brief introduction information that was not available before  the increase of information obviously helps the model to distinguish each book more easily  so that it can carry out more accurate classification prediction  and the brief introduction can better describe the characteristics of the book  generally  when we buy a book  we can learn the general content of the book through the brief introduction  the second is to replace a text processing model that is more suitable for extracting long text features  our previous model lacked the ability to handle long text features well  so we abandoned the brief introduction of books  with the development of nlp technology  a model that can effectively extract long text features has been developed  so we began to apply in our model to achieve the purpose we did not achieve before     our research also has some limitations  first of all  due to practical restrictions  we can only obtain part of the data of our school library and conduct research on this  the books collection structure of university libraries and other social libraries is usually different  these differences lead to our model being only applicable to university libraries with the book pre purchase scheme mentioned above  secondly  our model still has some unresolved problems  such as how to deal with the problem of new theme concept books  for example  for books with a metaverse concept that has never appeared before  our model may not choose to buy such books  but these books are very likely to be needed by the library  what is more  we may need to add more reader feedback to make book purchases more in line with readers  needs  but this requires collecting more and adding more readers  data     our model has a high accuracy rate and can already assist the book purchasing task to a certain extent  furthermore  based on the limitations of our model  there may be some research directions for improvement in the future  for example  how to deal with the issue of new books  perhaps we can change the model and add some new hot topics of the year as one of the predictive factors  for the question of more personalized book purchases  research can be carried out on the book data with reader feedback information  for the limitations for libraries  if we can obtain book purchase data from other libraries  we can conduct more research on the applicability of purchase models     conclusion  in this paper  we presented albert bpf  a transformer based book purchase model for libraries  we used the structure of albert to extract features of books  text information  then in the bpf module  we used the book feature vector extracted by albert to predict books which need to be purchased     the work in this paper is an improvement of our previous work  guo et al   2020   we used the transformer architecture  which has been successful in the nlp field in recent years  to improve the model s performance  with the powerful text feature extraction capabilities based on the transformer architecture  our model has achieved very good results  compared with our previous work  our model has a lot of improvement in accuracy and is more robust  judging from the experimental results  our model has been able to assist well manual book purchase methods   figures    figure 1  the framework of albert bpf      figure 2  model flowchart      figure 3  the line chart of albert bpf s and cnn book s accuracy  precision  recall and f1    table 1  an example of book with detailed information    feature value  isbn 978 7 5192 0017 6  price cny45 00  language english  title an introduction to econophysics  subtitle correlations and complexity in finance  authors rosario n  mantegna  brief introduction this book concerns the use of concepts from statistical physics in the description of financial systems  the authors illustrate the scaling concepts used in probability theory  critical phenomena and fully developed turbulent fluids  these concepts are then applied to financial time series  the authors also present a stochastic model that displays several of the statistical properties observed in empirical data   publisher beijing world publication corporation  place of publication beijing  year of publication 2015  target readers undergraduates and researchers in the fields of econophysics  experts in the field of finance  etc   topic words econophysics  table 2  the statistics of total book data and order data from 2010 to 2016    year volume of total volume of ratio of order data  book data order data to total book data  2010 162 121 34 092 21 03   2011 190 871 32 199 16 87   2012 201 621 26 655 13 22   2013 191 860 31 211 16 27   2014 184 696 31 153 16 87   2015 158 386 30 487 19 25   2016 163 184 35 558 21 79   table 3  the performance of albert bpf from 2010 to 2016    year accuracy precision recall f1  2010 85 11  82 39  89 31  85 71   2011 86 41  82 16  93 02  87 25   2012 88 06  83 42  95 01  88 84   2013 86 82  84 59  90 04  87 23   2014 85 09  79 53  94 49  86 37   2015 85 26  81 51  91 21  86 09   2016 8