differences between text retrieval and database retrieval what s the difference between the two  okay  so if we think about the information or data managed by the two systems  we will see that in text retrieval  the data is unstructured  it s free text  but in databases  they are structured data where there is a clear defined schema to tell you this column is the names of people and that column is ages  etc   play video starting at  4 31 and follow transcript4 31  the unstructured text is not obvious what are the names of people mentioned in the text   play video starting at  4 40 and follow transcript4 40  because of this difference  we also see that text information tends to be more ambiguous and we talk about that in the processing chapter  whereas in databases  but they don t tend to have where to find the semantics   play video starting at  4 58 and follow transcript4 58  the results important difference in the queries  and this is partly due to the difference in the information or data   play video starting at  5 7 and follow transcript5 07  so test queries tend to be ambiguous  whereas in their research  the queries are typically well defined  think about a sql query that would clearly specify what records to be returned  so it has very well defined semantics   play video starting at  5 27 and follow transcript5 27  keyword queries or electronic queries tend to be incomplete  also in that it doesn t really specify what documents should be retrieved  whereas complete specification for what should be returned   play video starting at  5 47 and follow transcript5 47  and because of these differences  the answers would be also different  being the case of text retrieval  we re looking for it rather than the documents   play video starting at  5 58 and follow transcript5 58  in the database search  we are retrieving records or match records with the sequel query more precisely   play video starting at  6 9 and follow transcript6 09  now in the case of text retrieval  what should be the right answers to the query is not very well specified  as we just discussed   play video starting at  6 21 and follow transcript6 21  so it s unclear what should be the right answers to a query  and this has very important consequences  and that is  textual retrieval is an empirically defined problem   play video starting at  6 38 and follow transcript6 38  so this is a problem because if it s empirically defined  then we can not mathematically prove one method is better than another method   play video starting at  6 52 and follow transcript6 52  that also means we must rely on empirical evaluation involving users to know which method works better   play video starting at  7 2 and follow transcript7 02  and that s why we have  you need more than one lectures to cover the issue of evaluation  because this is very important topic for sir jennings   play video starting at  7 13 and follow transcript7 13  without knowing how to evaluate heroism properly  there s no way to tell whether we have got the better or whether one system is better than another 
