campuswire post describing how zipf s law relates to compressing both term frequencies and document ids by using d gap  document gap   since large numbers are less frequent than small numbers  compressing with something like gamma or delta encoding provides us with substantial benefits document id s are stored via d gap  you subtract the document id that a term appears    did_id   i          in by the document id that the term last appeared in    di 1d_ i   1 d   i 1           then you store those d gap differences  there are thus two cases for when we are indexing document ids that a term occurs in     the term is a very common word that occurs a lot of the time  so it occurs in a lot of documents  in this case  we expect the d gap to be small for the documents it occurs in  since we are accessing sequentially   this is the most frequent case by far  again according to zipf s law  the term is a rare word and only occurs in a few documents  in this case  it is possible we get a large d gap value  however  this is rare and won t happen much  since only a few documents contain this query term to begin with  so we can leverage this skew in the term distributions when compressing values here as well
