 a paper further elaborating on weak supervision which allows us to work with a large corpus of unlabeled data  specifically  this section details how to approach labeling functions that users identify having correlations or dependence with one another  which is quite interesting one shortcoming of the above model is that it fails to account for the fact that labelling functions may be correlated with one another  for instance when a labelling function is computed from the output of another labeling function  to capture those dependencies  we extend the model with a weighting scheme   or equivalently  a tempering of the densities associated with each labelling function  formally  for each labelling function  j and observed label k we determine weights  wjk  with respect to which the corresponding densities of the labelling functions are annealed  this flattens to different degrees the underlying probabilities for the components of the multinomials  the observed process has then a tempered multinomial distribution with a density of form  p    i  j   yij  p si j   wj     y l k 1 p si jk yijkwjk    3  the temperatures  wjk  are determined using a scheme inspired by delution priors widely used in bayesian model averaging  george  1999  george et al   2010   the idea relies on redundancy as the measure of prior information on the importance of features  formally  we define for each  j a neighbourhood n  j   consisting of labelling functions known to be correlated with  j   as is the case for labelling functions built on top of another function s outputs  the weights are then specified as  wjk   exp       x l n  j   rjlk       4  where   is a hyper parameter specifying the strength of the weighting scheme  and rjlk is the recall between labelling functions  j and  l for label k  informally  the weight wjk of a labelling function  j producing the label k will decrease if  j exhibits a high recall with correlated sources  and is therefore at least partially redundant 
