explains more in detail what makes the formula for bm25  different than bm25 lv   zhai  11  go on to observe that the penalization of long documents occurs not only in bm25 but other ranking functions  they propose a general solution to this  which is to lower bound the contribution of a single term occurrence  that is  no matter how long the document  a single occurrence of a search term contributes at least a constant amount to the retrieval status value  they do this differently from bm25l  they add   to the tftd component before multiplying by idf  which they also change  
extract and parse data from html and xml beautiful soup is a python library for pulling data out of html and xml files  it works with your favorite parser to provide idiomatic ways of navigating  searching  and modifying the parse tree  
push and pull differentiated using actual examples pull mode  one of the simple example to see this mode of text data access is search engine  a user will always input the text or a query to the system and the system will try to find relevant media in context with a given search  so we can say that users take initiative in this mode  also  the system wants us to input certain information for further steps i e ad hoc information is needed for this mode of text access  the below figure is an example of this mode of accessing information       example of querying word  computer  in amazon  pull mode has two types querying and browsing    i will discuss querying and browsing after push mode     push mode  in this mode  the system takes initiative to give us the information or rather recommend us certain information for e g recommender systems of amazon  flipkart  netflix  etc  in this mode  the system has good knowledge about the user s need for their interest  and accordingly  the filtering is done to push the information to you  stable information is a need in this mode  unlike pull mode where ad hoc information is required  the below figure is an example of this mode of accessing information       example of amazon s recommendation system  source   let s discuss more about querying and browsing     querying vs browsing  let me explain the difference between both of these using a simple example  consider you reside in some place  a  and you want to go to a place  c   now if you know the path from  a  to  c  you will follow that definite path  but what if you don t know that place  c  but you know about a place  b  which is nearby the place  c  so you will go till place  b  and then ask any person to guide you to the path to  c  and then you will proceed  i know we can simply type the place name in google maps and will get the route   but i am neglecting that fact here  so did you observe something in two approaches  so let me tell you that when you are definite about the path you can simply get to that place  this is analogous to querying and when don t have a definite path you take intermediary steps which is analogous to browsing     let s go for some formal definition     querying  in querying users enter a query or keyword and system return relevant documents  querying is only possible when users are well aware of what query to use to get information  we can also understand this by simple sql query which returns relevant information upon a query statement     browsing  in browsing  the user navigates through certain relevant documents or information by certain paths  we do browsing when we want to explore certain topics or sometimes we do browsing when we don t know the exact keyword or query to use  to be able to get exact documents or media we need to do exact queries in a vast space of information  internet  we need a topical map to tour the information space which is not well defined or well structured as of now     i hope you are now able to distinguish between query and browsing and push and push modes     thank you     references   1  c  zhai and s  massung  text data management and analysis  a practical introduction to information retrieval and text mining  acm book series  morgan   claypool publishers  2016     60    
