how cosine similarity and euclidean distance are different from each other cosine similarity and euclidian distance are two different ways to measure vector similarity  the former measures the similarity of vectors with respect to the origin  while the latter measures the distance between particular points of interest along the vector  you can use either in isolation  combine them and use both  or look at one of many other ways to determine similarity  see these slides from a michael collins lecture for more info 
