explains information retrieval information retrieval  part 2   simplifying the information    in the previous article  we explored a technique to extract the information  let us now look at some of the key terms to understand before moving forward with the coding part     tokenization  given a character sequence and a defined document unit  tokenization is the task of chopping it up into pieces  called tokens   perhaps at the same time throwing away certain characters  such as punctuation     tokenization can be of two types     tokenizing paragraphs into sentences  e g  assume a given a document with the following text    sam is a good student  he is kind and helpful in nature      the tokenized sentences would be the list     sam is a good student     he is kind and helpful in nature       tokenizing sentences into individual words  e g  if we want to tokenize the first sentence of the above paragraph  the tokenized form would be the list    sam    is    a    good    student           stopwords  stopwords are a list of most commonly used in any natural language  for the purpose of analyzing text data and building nlp models  these stopwords might not add much value to the meaning of the document and hence we exclude them from analysis     if you want to view the list of stopwords in english  you can run the following code       lemmatization v s stemming  defining the two terms     stemming is a rudimentary rule based process for text normalization technique that cuts off the end or beginning of a word by taking into account a list of common prefixes or suffixes that could be found in that word    lemmatization is an organized method of obtaining the root form of the word  it makes use of vocabulary  dictionary importance of words  and morphological analysis  word structure and grammar relations      stemming is a fast but dirty method of chopping up the words     lemmatization is an intelligent operation that uses dictionaries of a particular language     hence  lemmatization helps in forming better features     inverted index  the inverted index of a document collection is basically a data structure that attaches each distinctive term with a list of all documents that contains the term     a modified version of it could be one in which we attach the frequency of the word in a particular document along with the document number  the image below would clarify the intuition 
inverted index construction  steps to build an inverted index     fetch the document   removing of stop words  stop words are most occurring and useless words in document like  i    the    we    is    an    stemming of root word   whenever i want to search for  cat   i want to see a document that has information about it  but the word present in the document is called  cats  or  catty  instead of  cat   to relate the both words  i ll chop some part of each and every word i read so that i could get the  root word   there are standard tools for performing this like  porter s stemmer    record document ids   if word is already present add reference of document to index else create new entry  add additional information like frequency of word  location of word etc   
text search algorithms  three classical techniques for text retrieval techniques have been defined for organizing items in a textual database  for rapidly identifying the relevant items and for eliminating items that do not satisfy the search  the techniques are full text scanning  streaming   word inversion and multi attribute retrieval  faloutsos 85  salton 83   in addition to using the indexes as a mechanism for searching text in information systems  streaming of text was frequently found in the systems as an additional search mechanism  streaming of text is a sequential search of the text  this technique is used to complete a query by searching for query terms that could not be satisfied by the index  e g   imbedded search terms   it is also frequently used to locate the search terms for highlighting in the retrieved item prior to display 
example of encoding in byte of text retrieval v byte encoding allows us to use fewer bits to represent smaller numbers  which result from delta encoding   while allowing arbitrarily large numbers in the index  we use 7 bits of each byte to represent the number itself  and 1 bit to indicate how many bytes are used to represent the full number 
