summary of tr evaluation to summarize the whole part of textual evaluation  it s extremely important  because the problem is the empirically defined problem  if we  play video starting at  12 38 and follow transcript12 38  don t rely on users  there s no way to tell whether one method works better   play video starting at  12 43 and follow transcript12 43  if we have in the property experiment design  we might misguide our research or applications  and we might just draw wrong conclusions  and we have seen this is in some of our discussions  so make sure to get it right for your research or application   play video starting at  13  and follow transcript13 00  the main methodology is the cranfield evaluation methodology  and they are the main paradigm used in all kinds of empirical evaluation tasks  not just a search engine variation  map and ndcg are the two main measures that you should definitely know about and they are appropriate for comparing ranking algorithms  you will see them often in research papers  precision at 10 documents is easier to interpret from user s perspective  so that s also often useful   play video starting at  13 30 and follow transcript13 30  what s not covered is some other evaluation strategy like a b test
explanation of accumulator an accumulator is nothing but a counter that you keep for the documents when trying to compute the total relevance score for each document  so that the documents with the highest relevance scores are returned as an answer to the query in end      when evaluating a query  we look at each term of the query  and at each term  we check the documents that the term is present  we increment the accumulator for all those documents  this is an iterative process done for all the terms in the query  and finally  the documents with the highest scores in the accumulator are returned as results     accumulator is discussed in lecture 2 6  the explanation is sometime after the 3rd minute   you could also take a look at page 154 of the textbook where you can see the pseudocode showing how an accumulator can be populated     now  based on this knowledge  if we have a word w1 that is present in 1000 documents  how many accumulators do you think we would need for this word 
