article detailing the cranfield methodology for evaluating ml systems offline  describes test collection creation with depth k pooling and contrasts this paradigm with a b testing   consider evaluating n content classifiers such as a  b  c   n  we have access production traffic   consider a real time data stream of content     collect a large enough random sample of content from production traffic  this is our test collection  how much to sample   say  the prevalence of your dataset is 1   to collect 10k positive samples you would need  10  x 10    10 million examples    one of the simplifying assumptions made by the cranfield paradigm is that the entire test collection needs to be labeled for pairwise comparison  when the test collection is large  consider millions  labeling all examples in test collection is difficult due to the immense human labor required   in order to avoid labeling the entire test collection depth k pooling is used  each ml system scores each examples and the examples are sorted based on these prediction values  only the top k examples are chosen to be labeled  examples outside of this pool are not considered 
