knowledge based vsm for clustering documents this paper presents a new knowledge based vector space model  vsm  for text clustering  in the new model  semantic relationships between terms  e g   words or concepts  are included in representing text documents as a set of vectors  the idea is to calculate the dissimilarity between two documents more effectively so that text clustering results can be enhanced  in this paper  the semantic relationship between two terms is defined by the similarity of the two terms  such similarity is used to re weight term frequency in the vsm  we consider and study two different similarity measures for computing the semantic relationship between two terms based on two different approaches  the first approach is based on the existing ontologies like wordnet and mesh  we define a new similarity measure that combines the edge counting technique  the average distance and the position weighting method to compute the similarity of two terms from an ontology hierarchy  the second approach is to make use of text corpora to construct the relationships between terms and then calculate their semantic similarities  three clustering algorithms  bisecting k means  feature weighting k means and a hierarchical clustering algorithm  have been used to cluster real world text data represented in the new knowledge based vsm  the experimental results show that the clustering performance based on the new model was much better than that based on the traditional term based vsm  keywords text clustering   knowledge based vsm   term similarity   semantic relationship part of this work was done in the university of hong kong and hong kong baptist university  l  jing  b  school of computer and information technology  beijing jiaotong university  beijing  china e mail  lpjinghk gmail com m  k  ng department of mathematics  hong kong baptist university  kowloon tong  hong kong  china j  z  huang e business technology institute  the university of hong kong  pokfulam road  hong kong  china 123 36 l  jing et al  1 introduction clustering text documents into categories is an important step in indexing  retrieval  management and mining of abundant text data on the web or in corporate information systems  a well known challenge in text clustering is handling of text data in large volume  high dimensionality and complex semantics  although some clustering algorithms can be applied to large and high dimensional text data  see  1  for a early survey and recent work  2 7    effectively dealing with complex semantics is still a remaining problem  in text clustering  a set of documents can be represented in a matrix x in which each row xi indicates a document and each column a unique term word tj   each element xi j represents the weight of term tj in document xi and is usually given as the frequency of occurrences of the term in the document  this representation is named as vector space model  vsm  in information retrieval  8   this simple vsm model cannot present semantics well because all columns  terms  are considered independent  however  many terms in text are semantically conceptually related  for example   suggestion  and  advice  can have the same meaning in a document but they will be represented as two independent terms  this situation can significantly affect the dissimilarity calculation between two documents  and therefore affect clustering results  indeed  the vsm model ignores all important semantic conceptual relations of words  such as synonymy  specialisation generalisation and part whole relationships in forming the representation matrix from a set of documents  recently  steinbach et al   9  examined that a document is often full of cluster independent general words and short of cluster dependent core words  it is very important to increase the importance of cluster dependent core words and reduce the contribution of cluster independent general words so that the dissimilarity between two documents can be calculated more accurately in text clustering and good clustering results can be obtained  in this paper  we propose a knowledge based vsm to represent documents with embedding of semantic conceptual relationship information of words in the representation matrix  different from the early work on aggregation of words into concept levels  10 12   we embed the semantic relationship information directly in the weights of the corresponding words which are semantically related by readjusting the weight values through similarity measures  our idea is to re adjust word weights so that the importance of cluster dependent core words can be increased and the contribution of cluster independent general words can be reduced  we propose two methods to measure the semantic similarity between a pair of terms  one uses a given ontology and the other calculates the similarity from a text corpus  the ontology method treats the terms as the nodes in an ontology hierarchy  and computes the semantic similarity between two nodes as the weighted distance between the locations of the two nodes in the ontology hierarchy  the corpus method calculates the semantic similarity between two terms by measuring the context information  this method is useful in the situation where a proper ontology is not available  based on different similarity measures  different knowledge based vsms can be constructed to represent text data  based on constructed knowledge based vsms  the dissimilarity between two documents can be calculated more accurately in text clustering and good clustering results can be obtained  to demonstrate the effectiveness of the new knowledge based vsm in text clustering  we have conducted a series of experiments on real text data with several clustering algorithms  in these experiments  four different document term representation methods were used  including the vsm  knowledge based vsm and other two representation methods  the clustering results have shown significant improvements on clustering accuracy when the text data was represented in the new model  the results also show that the new term similarity measures defined in this paper performed better than those proposed in  13   these results 123 knowledge based vector space model for text clustering 37 have further shown that the knowledge based vsm and the new similarity measures can indeed improve text clustering  the rest of the paper is organized as follows  we reviewed related work in sect  2  the framework of the knowledge based vsm is presented in sect  3  the knowledge based term similarity measures are discussed in sect  4  experimental results are given in sect  5 to illustrate the effectiveness of the knowledge based vsms to improve the performance of the clustering algorithms  and also show that the new knowledge based term similarity is more reasonable than the exiting measures  finally  we give some concluding remarks in sect  6
