bag of words with word just count implementation in python the most simple and known method is the bag of words representation  it s an algorithm that transforms the text into fixed length vectors  this is possible by counting the number of times the word is present in a document  the word occurrences allow to compare different documents and evaluate their similarities for applications  such as search  document classification  and topic modeling
instructions for installing metapy on m1 mac  for anyone facing the issue of installing metapy on their mac m1 m1 pro machines  i believe there are a couple of reasons     metapy does not work after python 3 7 and most of the mac machines come with python 3 8  installed   the metapy package requires  cmake  to be installed and it is not found   the easiest way to resolve both of these issues is to install anaconda and use it to both install cmake as well as get the right python version  here are the steps that worked for me 
provides overall context behind text retrieval systems generic data management software  stuart ferguson  rodney hebels  in computers for librarians  third edition   2003    types of text retrieval system  the main text retrieval systems to consider fall into one of three main categories        structured text retrieval systems  such as the system used to search online hosts like dialog       unstructured text retrieval systems       image retrieval systems  that store images of documents in machine readable form and link these to index files containing surrogate records     structured text retrieval systems  the  structure  referred to here is the file structure discussed in earlier chapters  in which computer files  such as files stored on online hosts or reference type cd roms  are organised into individual records  each record describing an information resource  for example  a monograph or a journal article  and each record consisting of fields  in this case  specific elements of bibliographic data  such as author  title and abstract  such records are sometimes described as surrogates  because they only represent the resources to which they refer  it is worth remembering  however  that the full text commercial databases described in chapter 2 will also be structured  and differ from bibliographic files only in having fields containing the text of the documents to which the bibliographic data refer     structured text retrieval systems are well known to librarians through the use of large scale commercial products  but they are also used by many small libraries to store and retrieve their bibliographic data  some inhouse systems use essentially the same retrieval software as the large scale systems  for example  the brs search software package used in some special libraries  some of these systems are also used in archives and records management     unstructured text retrieval systems  as the name suggests  unstructured text retrieval systems are those which  unlike the systems just described  do not organise data into records and fields  these are used to search the full text of documents that have been stored in electronic format  such as word processed documents that have been created internally by an organisation or that have been transmitted electronically from another organisation  typical applications fall into the records management area in the corporate sector  for example  the storage and retrieval of medical  legal  personnel and financial records     image retrieval systems  like both systems above  these can store the full text of documents  but the documents in this case are stored as images  not as retrievable text  pages from documents are scanned using special image scanning equipment  see the next chapter  and are stored in exactly the same way as  say  a page of graphics  this means that documents cannot be retrieved by searching for text in the documents  unless a further process is undertaken   see the account of optical character recognition in the following chapter   when the documents are scanned  they must also be indexed in the same way that paper based or microform documents are indexed  in other words  a surrogate record is created for each document  the surrogate record may contain data elements such as titles  names  dates  classification codes and subject descriptors  similar to the data elements that appear in a library catalogue     like the unstructured text retrieval systems  applications are generally of more interest to the records manager than to the library manager  nonetheless  image retrieval may be of interest to libraries that need to store specific kinds of document on a computer system  for example  a press clippings file  original manuscripts  departmental and organisational memos or  in the case of an academic library  educational and research documents  note also that  following records management practice   document  may also refer to non textual resources  for example  large pictorial collections  such as those in national and state libraries  may be scanned and stored on a high capacity storage medium  typically an optical disk of some kind  see chapter 6      view chapter  purchase book  information sources and services  stuart ferguson  rodney hebels  in computers for librarians  third edition   2003    creating an inhouse database  some commercial library management systems  still to be described  chapter 4   provide modules which can be used to construct inhouse databases  many  for example  include community information modules  which obviously require different fields from those used in bibliographic files  for example  names of organisations  descriptors  addresses  and contact names and numbers   the other common option is for libraries to develop databases using generic software  that is  software that is not library specific   particularly text retrieval systems  which have been mentioned already  and database management systems   the difference between the two types of system will be explored in chapter 5      the decisions which need to be made when developing an inhouse database include        what kind of system is most suitable for the application  for example  a text retrieval system or a database management system       what information to include  for example  whether or not to supply abstracts with an index  the decision will be affected by memory available on the computer system        whether or not the information can be organised in fields  a field structure might be suitable for an index but not necessarily for all applications        what length fields should be   may not be applicable for some software  for example  most text retrieval systems have variable length fields       what to do about repeating elements  such as subject descriptors  which may be given separate fields or may be listed together within the one field  see below    another decision that may be determined by the choice of software       which fields to use for retrieval   another decision affected by memory  since the indexes used for retrieval will use significant amounts of memory       whether or not to use a thesaurus for controlled vocabulary  and if so  whether to use a commercial one or to construct an inhouse product       sign in to download full size image  figure 2 10  treatment of repeating elements    view chapter  purchase book  definition of mir methodology  roberto raieli  in multimedia information retrieval  2013    summary of the fundamental principles and design of mir theory  the reference context of mir is composed of traditional systems of storage and retrieval of textual  visual  audio and audio visual documents  current systems that apply to the management of new multimedia documents  and different theoretical and technical efforts for the development of a more advanced and effective system for the managing and disseminating of digital documentation  this background is varied and dynamic  involving diverse professional work  and can be interconnected by creating a simple and effective information tool  to serve the needs of as many users as possible     within this vast scope of library sciences  documentation and technology  mir anchors itself in various ways to results achieved in areas not only related to lis by following and respecting its principles  inside the organic subset of mir is a further complexity  composed of its organs endowed with specific theoretical  technical and applicative aspects  and suited to every kind of multimedia document search  tr  vr  vdr  and ar     the reduced effectiveness and lack of usefulness  above all with respect to the new needs of users  of the exclusively terminological research systems applied to new and advanced multimedia databases has been discussed  the differences in principle between the ir system of treatment and retrieval by terms of textual  visual  audio visual and audio documents  and mir s handling and search through words  images  movements and sounds to documents of any multimedia genre can now be determined  it is now clear in what sense the technology and method of mir  consistent with the concrete content of documents processed  can be defined as content based  as opposed to traditional systems based on descriptor terms for this content  known as term based     at the risk of repeating oneself  it is necessary to go back and summarize the basics of this revolution that mir must impose  in the indexing and search architectures based on term based logic  the search is defined textually  and captures multimedia documents only through its textual surrogates in the form of classification or indexing terms  subjects  titles or captions  everything is thus translated into terms  the document  its contents and the access keys that identify it  and as a result even the structure of the search expression must be translated  in multimedia searches  a more effective system is one that can operate almost directly on the contents  on the objective data relative to words  forms  movements  and sounds  in this structure the formulation of the request does not have to be forced within the boundaries of language  but can be freely brought to the system spontaneously by the user via words  forms  sounds  and movements  and immediately analyzed and satisfied     the solution that the technological state of content based systems gives to the different issues regarding multimedia search are based on the objective content of the document  the object of the treatment is not a nominal content  consisting of linguistic surrogates  but the substantial content  consisting of shapes and structures  phrases  lines  paths  sound  or better  by the mathematical data constituting them  content can be defined concretely  and is immediately representative of an object  not to be understood interpretatively and almost abstractly in its traditional meaning     this does not mean the rejection of every possible interpretation and conceptual representation of the documented content and the document itself  in consideration of the semantic limits of content based systems  an appropriate intellectual intervention in the search is often necessary to define meanings in addition to feelings  to specify query strategy and increase chances of recovery  this need to integrate contentual and semantic approaches to documents must lead to the definition of a unique system of multimedia information treatment  able to simultaneously consider information needs of an intellectual interpretive and contentual objective nature     the solution to the confrontation of the seemingly opposed semantic and contentual fields can only be the organic integration of the principles and methods of analysis  indexing  search and retrieval  the technologically advanced tools of mir have the task of allowing users to apply all their human skills  allowing an organic integration of intellectual and sensitive means  in essence it is a blanket  organic approach  which is valid for all types of multimedia documents  able to take uniquely into account their concrete and conceptual representability  in fact  documents of any nature can always be introduced into logical spaces in relation to each other through the support of net ontologies and in addition can be searchable without influences into these semantic positions using mir methods specific to each case  and in turn  organically interrelated     it is limiting to continue to set the search for information in generic ir terms  conducting any research under the conditions of a search through textual language  what must be considered is a broader criterion for mir  where every kind of digital document is treated  stored and recovered via language elements  or a meta language most suited to the specific nature of what is documented     mir s functional requirements  condensing the ensemble of research and applications related to mir  ramified in various areas and landing several achievements in the context of studies on multimedia documentation  two models of systems indicating the theoretical and technical state can now be distinguished     table 5 1  comparison between ir and mir systems    ir systems of term based ir  based on terminological information  from classification schemes and subject cataloguing  thesauri  natural language  and from full text  for the treatment and search of textual documents  and usually also of multimedia documents   mir systems of content based ir or mir  distinguishable in   vr visual retrieval systems  in which still image files are treated and searched via visual data proper to the same files  colours  lines  contours  structures  forms  orientations  spatial distribution  etc     vdr video retrieval systems  where audio visual handling is conducted with audio visual language  shots  objects moving in the frame  figurative variations  editing cuts  soundtrack  etc     ar audio retrieval systems  in which sound information is searched in terms of sound  tempo  pitches  frequencies rhythms  timbres  melodies  etc     in perspective  within the comprehensive ambit of the organic system of mir  fully realized according to its own principles  only mir systems can be taken into serious consideration     an understanding of the meaning and benefits of the mir systems passes through awareness and practice of the fact that the language of document processing and database searching should be adaptable in each instance  and consistent with the language in which the content of the documents sought is expressed     table 5 2  the mir system    mir systems of mir  in which content based characteristics naturally cover those which are term based  given that the work on the content does not distinguish deductively if it is textual  visual  audio or any other  specializing in   tr text retrieval systems  in which the same principle of content based processing legitimises and guarantees the use of textual data for searching textual information  words from full text  names  titles  dates  phrases  etc     vr visual retrieval systems  in which still image files are treated and searched via visual data proper to the same files  colours  lines  contours  structures  forms  orientations  spatial distribution  etc     vdr video retrieval systems  where audio visual handling is conducted with audio visual language  shots  objects moving in the frame  figurative variations  editing cuts  soundtrack  etc     ar audio retrieval systems  in which sound information is searched in terms of sound  tempo  pitch  frequency of rhythms  timbres  melodies  etc     with the exception of the need to intervene with external interpretation support to semantically epitomize operations  content based methods in mir allow handling and searching documents through appropriate means and appropriate logic in the same language as the information content        similarity        recognition        ratios of measures and values     using such search and retrieval keys as        shapes  structures        words  figures  movements  sounds        letters  lines  colours  patterns  tempo  notes     in this way the most advanced mir systems can be very helpful in supporting theoretical research and practical creativity  and it is as much a professional tool as a guide for common users  requests can be made simply and freely by sending an image or sound sample  with or without conceptual clarifications through parameters or texts  and the system finds documents having similar characteristics  the recovered documents can be shown along with other related ones  such as critical notes  technical tables  cards  references and links to similar documents  the users can fully apply their intelligence and sensitivity and their creative and imaginative skills  interacting with a system designed to accommodate even unpredictable variations in a search path  and to learn the users  strategies from their behaviour     the outlines currently being trialled in the design and application of mir systems can be presented in tables     table 5 3  database and index creation      analysis  identification of the elements in the document s content     data filing  creation of a general data file of each document     characterization  extraction of characteristic data relating to key aspects of the content and linking to the general data file     indexing  updating the index with contentual characteristics and general data     description and classification  connection of appropriate terminological information   table 5 4  search and retrieval process      preliminary search  terminological search to select a portion of the documents from the whole database     multimedia search  using some of the documents extracted as an example model to launch the multimedia search     similarity matching  automatic capture of documents whose resemblance to the example is to a degree included within the set parameters     elaboration  use of additional extracted documents  modifying characteristics  selection of parts  association of elements  terminological clarification  to relaunch the search   table 5 5  advanced functions      analysis at time of search  carried out automatically  and not only when creating or updating the database     external models  using samples taken from outside of the database     model composition  possibility to freely produce a search template through system functions   regarding all the above organic complexity of mir methodologies  it is always necessary to remember that to achieve a great precision of document recovery from a multimedia database  the coexistence of all means of retrieval is essential  with a large role for those based on terms  terminological searches can  first of all  be a valuable preliminary method in selecting a part of the documents in an archive  and in focussing the search on data such as classes  titles  and authors  subsequently  it may constitute a system of cleaning the inevitable noise specific to a content based search  specifying those degrees of semantic interpretation that the automated system is unable to directly detect in the analysis of the representative content characteristics of a document     all procedures can operate in constant and organic interaction  in a single system with a single search interface  composing a search formula combining words  images and sounds  serving to retrieve even complex documents whose content extends to all levels of sense and meaning  such search structuring allows users  again and again to employ  freely and flexibly alongside tools operating directly on concrete content  even conceptual or ontological outlines  so they are able to take into account their own sensibility  or their specialized or diffused  scholarly or common aptitudes  to classify information and documents in the context of any type of resources       sign in to download full size image  figure 5 1  the organic mir system1    everything so far established around mir can be partly exemplified through the illustration of some model passages of a vr system  clearly  in databases of mainly textual documents  the most appropriate and direct access keys are textual  i e  words and phrases  or terms and codes of the same linguistic and expressive nature of the document s content  see fig  5 2        sign in to download full size image  figure 5 2  example of a textual textual search2    in image archives  however  it appears rather hasty and unjustified to give only a textual description to the content of other forms of expression  see fig  5 3   this is as ineffective as it would be unproductive in the opposite case  i e  searching via pictures for textual content  see fig  5 4        sign in to download full size image  figure 5 3  example of a textual visual search3      sign in to download full size image  figure 5 4  example of a visual textual search4    the increased effectiveness of vr systems is based on the general content based method of mir  treating almost directly the objective content of documents  and outpacing traditional systems based solely on terms describing this content  the system  therefore  makes it possible to find images via the appropriate visual language  using items such as figures or parts thereof  their design  colours  and styles  see fig  5 5  
